window.MNLCP_DATA.search = [{"page": "About the Plan", "title": "At a glance", "url": "plan.html#at-a-glance", "text": "Lead agency: Hawaiʻi Division of Forestry and Wildlife (DOFAW), led by DOFAW Maui Authority: DOFAW is charged with conserving the State's native species and ecosystems under Chapter 195D, Hawaiʻi Revised Statutes Partners: U.S. Geological Survey (USGS), U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service (USFWS), University of Hawaiʻi at Mānoa, University of Hawaiʻi at Hilo, the Bishop Museum, and other land managers and scientists Geography: Maui Nui: the islands of Maui, Molokaʻi, Lānaʻi, and Kahoʻolawe Scope: native terrestrial and freshwater biodiversity; 442 at-risk species in eight taxonomic groups, with sev"}, {"page": "About the Plan", "title": "Purpose and need", "url": "plan.html#purpose-and-need", "text": "The MNLCP has three complementary objectives: prevent the extinction of the most at-risk native plants and animals, restore all at-risk species to healthy, self-sustaining populations, and restore Maui Nui's native ecosystems so they provide habitat for at-risk species and other native species. Maui Nui spans high volcanic peaks; mesic, wet, and dry forests; subalpine shrublands; wetlands; streams; and coastal dunes. These habitats sustain hundreds of rare, threatened, and endangered species, most of which occur nowhere else on Earth. The plan's analysis covers all lands. Where possible, it fo"}, {"page": "About the Plan", "title": "A global crisis, felt most on islands", "url": "plan.html#a-global-crisis-felt-most-on-islands", "text": "Global biodiversity is being lost at rates unprecedented in human history. Island ecosystems are among the most vulnerable, and the Hawaiian Islands show how severe the crisis can be. Hundreds of native Hawaiian species have already gone extinct, and hundreds more are at risk. Maui Nui alone has 442 native plant and animal species that experts consider at risk of extinction in the near future. Their loss would erode not only biological diversity but also cultural heritage, ecosystem function, and economic well-being."}, {"page": "About the Plan", "title": "How Hawaiʻi got here", "url": "plan.html#how-hawaiʻi-got-here", "text": "The Hawaiian Islands lie roughly 2,100 miles from the nearest continent. Long isolation produced remarkable endemism: about 90 percent of terrestrial species are found nowhere else. Polynesian settlement , around a thousand years ago, brought agriculture, non-native species, and fire, and began the decline of native lowland ecosystems, including the loss of almost all of Hawaiʻi's flightless birds. After Western contact in the late eighteenth century, ranching, plantation agriculture, commercial forestry, and urban development destroyed native forests, drained wetlands, diverted streams, and i"}, {"page": "About the Plan", "title": "Effort is growing, but not fast enough", "url": "plan.html#effort-is-growing-but-not-fast-enough", "text": "Conservation capacity in Hawaiʻi has grown, with more funding, more protected land, and more intensive management. Yet many species continue to decline, including hundreds with fewer than 50 individuals left in the wild. Current approaches are not keeping pace with the scale, complexity, and urgency of the crisis."}, {"page": "About the Plan", "title": "Why existing plans are not enough", "url": "plan.html#why-existing-plans-are-not-enough", "text": "Hawaiʻi has many high-quality plans: species recovery plans, the State Wildlife Action Plan, site management plans, and broader strategies. They provide essential scientific guidance, but they are not integrated at the landscape or management level. Managers must reconcile dozens of documents, each with different assumptions, scales, and objectives, on limited budgets and staff. This makes it hard to see multi-species opportunities, hard to prioritize, and slows progress toward preventing extinctions and restoring ecosystems. Even a relatively small reserve such as the Makawao Forest Reserve h"}, {"page": "About the Plan", "title": "What the MNLCP changes", "url": "plan.html#what-the-mnlcp-changes", "text": "Led by DOFAW Maui with a broad coalition of public and private agencies, organizations, landowners, and technical experts, the MNLCP combines three things: spatial prioritization (where to focus land management), action prioritization (which conservation actions matter most), and ecosystem management that benefits all Maui Nui biodiversity. For the first time, this gives Maui Nui an islands-wide, landscape-scale roadmap showing where conservation can most effectively prevent extinctions, recover species, and restore ecosystems. Instead of working species by species or site by site, the plan se"}, {"page": "About the Plan", "title": "Applications and use", "url": "plan.html#applications-and-use", "text": "The MNLCP is both a decision-support framework and a practical roadmap for implementation. It helps land managers: Prioritize actions based on effectiveness and efficiency Estimate and plan for costs across species and sites Identify lands that should be managed under long-term protection Design integrated work plans that deliver several conservation benefits at one site Coordinate actions across jurisdictions using shared data For the wider conservation community, it sets out the full set of actions needed to prevent extinctions and recover all at-risk species in Maui Nui. It also supports wo"}, {"page": "About the Plan", "title": "A path forward", "url": "plan.html#a-path-forward", "text": "The plan's overarching goals are to prevent further extinctions, recover species now at risk, and stabilize ecosystems so they keep providing habitat for all native plants and animals, along with public benefits: clean water, food security, cultural value, and natural beauty. Hawaiʻi's native species and habitats are linked to the state's ecological health, biocultural heritage, and economic prosperity. They form the foundation of cultural practices, natural resource management, and local economies."}, {"page": "About the Plan", "title": "Three integrated goals", "url": "plan.html#three-integrated-goals", "text": "Taken together, these goals protect the full breadth of Maui Nui's biodiversity: Prevent extinctions. Restore all currently at-risk species to healthy, self-sustaining population levels. Protect all remaining native ecosystems and restore degraded systems to provide enough habitat for all native Maui Nui biodiversity."}, {"page": "About the Plan", "title": "Planning frameworks and approaches", "url": "plan.html#planning-frameworks-and-approaches", "text": "The MNLCP is an initial application of Integrated Multi-Scale Conservation Planning (IMCP). IMCP brings species recovery planning, spatial prioritization, threat-based action prioritization, ecosystem restoration planning, and adaptive implementation into one decision-support system that works across spatial, ecological, and management scales. Teams of experts on Hawaiian plants, animals, ecosystems, and land management guided the plan. They were supported by analytic teams of research scientists from USGS, USFWS, UH Mānoa, UH Hilo, and the Bishop Museum. The building blocks, in order: Recover"}, {"page": "About the Plan", "title": "Procedural methods", "url": "plan.html#procedural-methods", "text": "A project of this scope does not suit a species-by-species or one-size-fits-all approach. Because nothing of this scale and complexity had been attempted before, the plan uses a variety of methods, including several new or highly modified approaches. The Approach page describes them."}, {"page": "About the Plan", "title": "A 20-year horizon", "url": "plan.html#a-20-year-horizon", "text": "The MNLCP is large and complex, and it is designed for a 20-year implementation period. Its strategies contain many actions that must be carried out across large areas, so it cannot be done everywhere at once. To help, species experts recommended an implementation sequence for each taxonomic group, and the plan identifies species facing imminent extinction that need action first. At-risk species are those likely to become extinct within the next 20 years without significant management intervention, and the action prioritization measures success as the chance of viable, self-sustaining populati"}, {"page": "About the Plan", "title": "A living plan", "url": "plan.html#a-living-plan", "text": "The MNLCP is not meant to be a one-time, static document. It is a flexible, manager-driven, expert-informed decision-support system that helps land and species managers coordinate actions, identify priority areas, recommend implementable actions, set performance metrics, and build a foundation for adaptive management, budgeting, staffing, and partnership development. Together with the Adaptive Conservation Planner, the plan is a living system that managers can update as circumstances and funding change, as opportunities arise, and as new information comes in. It supports both long-range planni"}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Integrated Multi-Scale Conservation Planning", "url": "approach.html#integrated-multi-scale-conservation-planning", "text": "The MNLCP is not only a spatial prioritization exercise or a standalone framework. It is a state-led, expert-engaged plan with several parts, deliberately combined into one flexible decision-support system. The MNLCP is an initial application of IMCP. The defining characteristics of IMCP are: State-led and practitioner-driven , rather than researcher-directed or top-down mandates. Expert-engaged , with specialists contributing qualitative judgment through open-ended, collaborative review. Integrated across scales: biologically, from populations to species, whole taxonomic groups, and ecosystem"}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "How the planning processes fit together", "url": "approach.html#how-the-planning-processes-fit-together", "text": "IMCP sits above, and includes, each analytical part of the plan: IMCP is the overarching planning approach. Spatial prioritization is carried out through the Transparent, Flexible, Expert-engaged Systematic Conservation Planning (TFE-SCP) process. Systematic Conservation Planning (SCP) looks for a complementary network of places that together meets explicit conservation targets, rather than the best site for each species on its own. OptimTFE is the site-selection prioritization tool used within TFE-SCP for the quantitative steps. Conservation actions are evaluated through a modified Priority T"}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Spatial prioritization: where to manage", "url": "approach.html#spatial-prioritization-where-to-manage", "text": "The Spatial Prioritization Team, led by USGS, addresses \"where to manage\" for the recovery of all Maui Nui at-risk species. It uses the USGS-developed TFE-SCP process: a comprehensive planning process with distinct stages, each supported by custom-built tools and each involving input from biological experts and land managers. [{\"title\": \"Set an explicit recovery target\", \"text\": \"Planning starts with one species and its populations. For the plant Cyanea mceldowneyi, the recovery target is 10 populations of 400 individuals each: 4,000 plants in all.\"}, {\"title\": \"Map suitable habitat\", \"text\": "}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Two process pathways", "url": "approach.html#two-process-pathways", "text": "Which pathway a group followed depended on how many species it has and how much information exists on their habitat needs. Both build on existing species plans. The two TFE-SCP pathways Algorithmic pathway Expert-led pathway Groups Plants, Terrestrial arthropods, Terrestrial snails Forest birds, Seabirds, Waterbirds, Aquatic invertebrates When used Many species, real differences in habitat quality, and practical issues such as access and ongoing management Fewer focal species, and expert knowledge can set spatial priorities directly How OptimTFE generates many candidate footprints that each me"}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Action prioritization: what to do", "url": "approach.html#action-prioritization-what-to-do", "text": "The Action Prioritization Team, led by UH Mānoa, addresses \"what to do.\" It used a modified Priority Threat Management (PTM) process, a structured way to compare the expected costs, feasibility, and benefits of management strategies. DOFAW and UH Mānoa convened collaborative workshops in which roughly 100 land managers and scientists contributed professional judgment through open-ended discussions on the cost, feasibility, and benefit of strategies for 374 of Maui Nui's at-risk plants and animals. The Taxonomic Teams later identified an additional 68 at-risk species. The team and its experts: "}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Ecosystem prioritization", "url": "approach.html#ecosystem-prioritization", "text": "The ecosystem effort, led by UH Hilo, looks beyond individual species. Healthy native ecosystems keep more plants and animals from becoming at risk. They also help the 442 species already at risk recover and stay recovered by providing connectivity, buffers from threats, redundancy, and room to expand. The ecosystem effort identifies: remaining good-quality native ecosystems and unique community types; conservation strategies that will reach ecosystem goals; and conservation opportunities based on habitat quality, the conservation status of lands, and the importance of the area to at-risk spec"}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Implementation: when to do it", "url": "approach.html#implementation-when-to-do-it", "text": "DOFAW land managers, planners, and decision-makers were engaged throughout. The Implementation Team, led by DOFAW, started the planning, set management needs and objectives, and turned the results of the other efforts into a cohesive plan for implementation: the \"when to do it\" guidance. It also oversees a Managers' Handbook, a practical guide for DOFAW staff using MNLCP tools and outputs. The team includes the land managers who will carry out the plan, along with partner organizations. The plan cannot be carried out everywhere at once over its 20-year horizon, so timing matters: Emergency fir"}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Why TFE-SCP", "url": "approach.html#why-tfe-scp", "text": "Maui Nui's at-risk species face overlapping threats across shared landscapes. Planning for one species or one site at a time does not show where limited resources would help the most species. TFE-SCP generates many candidate footprints with a flexible algorithm, then chooses among them by weighing trade-offs in criteria the experts agreed on. Footprints account for habitat quality, accessibility, climate resilience, and management feasibility, and can be coordinated across groups and with action and ecosystem prioritization."}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Planning units", "url": "approach.html#planning-units", "text": "Maui Nui is divided into 1,400 planning units of about 2 km² each, based on watersheds, natural barriers, and elevation bands. Each unit is sized to support a single population of an at-risk species; exact siting within a unit is left to field teams. Terrestrial snails are the exception: their network is built from candidate exclosure sites, not standard planning units."}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Algorithmic pathway stages (Plants, Terrestrial arthropods, Terrestrial snails)", "url": "approach.html#algorithmic-pathway-stages-plants-terrestrial-arthropods-terrestrial-snails", "text": "Data gathering and vetting. Occurrence records, population locations, habitat suitability models, and planning-unit data are compiled in the Expert Data Platform; taxon experts validate inputs and flag errors or gaps. Candidate footprint generation. The OptimTFE R package builds a large set of candidate footprints, each meeting species-specific targets. Criteria exploration and trade-off analysis. Experts and managers compare candidates across criteria and pick a draft footprint that balances competing values. Planning-unit review. Planning units and species targets in poor habitat are reviewe"}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Expert-led pathway (Forest birds, Seabirds, Waterbirds, Aquatic invertebrates)", "url": "approach.html#expert-led-pathway-forest-birds-seabirds-waterbirds-aquatic-invertebrates", "text": "Specialists drew priority areas directly through repeated web-map review, using the same Expert Data Platform and Conservation Planner but without OptimTFE candidate footprints. This suits groups with few focal species and strong existing expert knowledge."}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Data inputs", "url": "approach.html#data-inputs", "text": "Experts reviewed and refined each input before use. Species occurrence and population data. Delineated populations for Plants where available, vetted records otherwise; modern and historical records for Terrestrial snails; modern records plus host-plant associations for Terrestrial arthropods. Taxon experts vetted all occurrence data in collaborative review sessions. Habitat suitability and climate envelopes. Habitat suitability models for Plants; expert-reviewed climate-similarity models for Terrestrial snails; host-plant distributions and broad climate envelopes for Terrestrial arthropods. H"}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Selection criteria", "url": "approach.html#selection-criteria", "text": "No single metric, and not the algorithm alone, chose the final footprint. Candidates that all met species targets were compared on nine criteria, and each group weighted them differently: Footprint area (fewer planning units concentrates resources) Habitat quality Mean habitat suitability of target species Share in biodiversity reserves Share under active ungulate control Accessibility Fire risk Climate resilience Overlap with other groups' footprints (shared management)"}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Footprint types", "url": "approach.html#footprint-types", "text": "Footprints were produced for each of the seven focal groups and can be viewed alone or combined as a multi-taxon \"mega-footprint.\" Appendix 1 names recovery footprints, priority footprints (nested subsets of recovery footprints), and implementation subsets that guide near-term staging. Every footprint keeps species-level detail: which species each unit was chosen for, each species' habitat suitability, and whether the choice rests on occurrence records or model predictions."}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Results by group (Appendix 1)", "url": "approach.html#results-by-group-appendix-1", "text": "Spatial prioritization by taxonomic group, as described in Appendix 1 Group Pathway Emphasis or method Products Plants Algorithmic Habitat quality, biodiversity reserves, ungulate management, compact footprint Recovery footprint of 317 PUs; priority footprint of 181 PUs with up to three recovery populations per species where feasible Terrestrial snails Algorithmic (exclosure sites) Exclosure count, accessibility, fire risk, climate resilience Network of 32 exclosure sites with three targets per species; priority network of 14 sites giving each of the 49 species one target Terrestrial arthropod"}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Using the footprints", "url": "approach.html#using-the-footprints", "text": "In the Conservation Planner, each planning unit or exclosure site shows its target species, habitat scores, the evidence behind it, and expert notes on why it was selected. All group footprints can be viewed together; where they overlap, actions for one group can benefit others. The arthropod footprint overlaps substantially with the plant footprint, so many arthropod sites benefit from work already planned for plant recovery."}, {"page": "The Approach", "title": "Limitations noted in the plan", "url": "approach.html#limitations-noted-in-the-plan", "text": "Occurrence data are sparse for many snail taxa; the network should be updated as surveys find more populations. The snail network does not address landscape-scale threats. Some arthropod species may have no targets until surveys provide defensible recovery locations. Some plant species remain below target because suitable sites or known populations are lacking; these gaps are documented."}, {"page": "Partners & Teams", "title": "Why teams matter", "url": "partners.html#why-teams-matter", "text": "The MNLCP uses a multi-scale planning approach that combines three kinds of teams: teams that oversee plan development and communications; subject matter specialists and the land managers who will carry out the plan; and specialized analytic teams that used custom-designed prioritization processes to work out where to focus land management, which conservation actions matter most for species and ecosystems, and where key ecosystems can be protected and restored. Each team played an important and strategic role in developing and carrying out the plan. The plan's strength comes from the on-the-gr"}, {"page": "Partners & Teams", "title": "Core Team (DOFAW-led)", "url": "partners.html#core-team-dofaw-led", "text": "Organized by DOFAW, the Core Team brought together land managers, key leaders from the other teams, and a facilitator. It provided recommendations and organizational support to DOFAW and oversaw plan development, scheduling, and coordination of the other MNLCP teams."}, {"page": "Partners & Teams", "title": "Taxonomic Teams (DOFAW-led)", "url": "partners.html#taxonomic-teams-dofaw-led", "text": "Teams of species specialists were the irreplaceable backbone of the planning effort. DOFAW coordinated the taxon expert teams, and experts participated in collaborative review sessions throughout the process. They contributed their knowledge of which at-risk species to include, where those species occur, their habitat requirements and conservation needs, and how feasible management actions are. Seven Taxonomic Teams focused on seven key taxonomic groups: Forest birds Seabirds Waterbirds Plants Terrestrial snails Terrestrial arthropods Aquatic invertebrates The Hawaiian hoary bat (ʻōpeʻapeʻa), "}, {"page": "Partners & Teams", "title": "Spatial Prioritization Team (USGS-led)", "url": "partners.html#spatial-prioritization-team-usgs-led", "text": "This team addresses \"where to manage\" for the recovery of all Maui Nui at-risk species. The plan uses the USGS-developed Transparent, Flexible, Expert-engaged Systematic Conservation Planning (TFE-SCP) process, a comprehensive planning process with distinct stages, each supported by custom-built tools and involving input from biological experts and land managers. The work is state-led and practitioner-driven. DOFAW coordinates the expert teams and will implement the resulting conservation priorities; USGS provided the technical methods, prioritization tools, and spatial analysis support. The f"}, {"page": "Partners & Teams", "title": "Action Prioritization Team (UH Mānoa-led)", "url": "partners.html#action-prioritization-team-uh-mānoa-led", "text": "This team addresses \"what to do.\" Using a modified Priority Threat Management (PTM) process, it identified which management actions provide the greatest benefit for species recovery. DOFAW and UH Mānoa convened collaborative workshops in which roughly 100 land managers and scientists contributed professional judgment through open-ended discussions on the expected cost, feasibility, and benefit of different management strategies. Explore priority actions"}, {"page": "Partners & Teams", "title": "Ecosystem Prioritization (UH Hilo-led)", "url": "partners.html#ecosystem-prioritization-uh-hilo-led", "text": "The ecosystem effort, led by UH Hilo, identifies remaining good-quality native ecosystems and unique community types, conservation strategies to reach ecosystem goals, and conservation opportunities based on habitat quality, the conservation status of lands, and the importance of an area to at-risk species or other values. Explore ecosystem priorities"}, {"page": "Partners & Teams", "title": "Implementation Team (DOFAW-led)", "url": "partners.html#implementation-team-dofaw-led", "text": "DOFAW land managers, planners, and decision-makers have been engaged throughout the planning process. They are responsible for initiating planning, setting management needs and objectives, and turning the results of the other efforts into a cohesive plan for implementation: the \"when to do it\" guidance. The team also oversees a Managers' Handbook, a practical guide for DOFAW staff using MNLCP tools and outputs. The Implementation Team includes the land managers charged with carrying out the MNLCP, along with partner organizations. See how managers use the plan"}, {"page": "Partners & Teams", "title": "Communications Team", "url": "partners.html#communications-team", "text": "The Communications Team develops and carries out strategies to inform the public and others about the MNLCP and its accomplishments."}, {"page": "Partners & Teams", "title": "Lead agency", "url": "partners.html#lead-agency", "text": "Hawaiʻi Division of Forestry and Wildlife (DOFAW) , Department of Land and Natural Resources (DLNR). DOFAW is charged under Chapter 195D, Hawaiʻi Revised Statutes, with conserving the State's native species and ecosystems, in close partnership with federal agencies, non-governmental organizations, private landowners, and communities."}, {"page": "Partners & Teams", "title": "Analytic partners", "url": "partners.html#analytic-partners", "text": "Expert teams were supported by analytic teams of research scientists from: U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) , which led spatial prioritization University of Hawaiʻi at Mānoa (UH Mānoa) , which led action prioritization University of Hawaiʻi at Hilo (UH Hilo) , which led ecosystem prioritization U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service (USFWS) Bishop Museum"}, {"page": "Partners & Teams", "title": "Conservation programs named in the plan", "url": "partners.html#conservation-programs-named-in-the-plan", "text": "These programs are named in the plan for their role in specific parts of the work: Plant Extinction Prevention Program (PEPP) already addresses the plants facing imminent extinction, and its population data informed plant planning. PEPP partners already use one of the plan's tools to choose outplanting sites. Snail Extinction Prevention Program played a central role in developing the MNLCP snail strategy. Maui Forest Bird Recovery Project focuses on forest bird recovery and identified priority actions to prevent the extinction of kiwikiu and ʻākohekohe. Hawaiʻi and Pacific Plant Recovery Coord"}, {"page": "At-Risk Species", "title": "How at-risk species were identified", "url": "species.html#how-at-risk-species-were-identified", "text": "DOFAW coordinated taxon expert teams, called Taxonomic Teams, for each group of species. Working with other species experts, they reviewed species one by one. They drew on their own field knowledge, existing endangered species lists, Hawaiʻi's State Wildlife Action Plan, conservation databases, and the published literature."}, {"page": "At-Risk Species", "title": "The test: likely extinct within 20 years", "url": "species.html#the-test-likely-extinct-within-20-years", "text": "A species was considered at risk if it is likely to become extinct within the next 20 years without significant management. The main reasons species fall into this category are: very few individuals left steep declines in numbers or range caused by a changing climate newly introduced invasive species habitat loss"}, {"page": "At-Risk Species", "title": "Listed and not-yet-listed species", "url": "species.html#listed-and-not-yet-listed-species", "text": "State and federal endangered species lists already included 202 of these species as endangered or threatened. The other 240 were identified as at risk by the experts for each group. More than half of Maui Nui's at-risk species (240 of 442) are not on any endangered species list. Planning only for listed species would leave them out. At-risk species by taxonomic group, with listing status, species facing imminent extinction, and recovery or survey category (plan Table 1, p.19) Taxonomic group At-risk species Listed (State/ESA) Facing imminent extinction Interim recovery goals Full recovery goal"}, {"page": "At-Risk Species", "title": "Species facing imminent extinction", "url": "species.html#species-facing-imminent-extinction", "text": "Of the 442 at-risk species, 163 are so close to extinction that they may be lost within the next five years without immediate emergency action. They are: 102 Plants all 49 Terrestrial snails 9 Terrestrial arthropods, all yellow-faced bees ( Hylaeus ) 2 Forest birds, the kiwikiu and the ʻākohekohe 1 Aquatic invertebrate, the flying earwig Hawaiian damselfly ( Megalagrion nesiotes ) No Seabirds, Waterbirds, or Hawaiian hoary bats are expected to be lost within five years."}, {"page": "At-Risk Species", "title": "What emergency action means", "url": "species.html#what-emergency-action-means", "text": "Emergency actions are chosen for how well and how quickly they work. They often happen outside the wild (ex situ), such as captive rearing and seed storage. Others are close to the wild, such as very small predator-proof exclosures. Alongside these, the plan protects and manages key wild populations so that each species survives through the life of the plan. Most species that need emergency measures already have interim or full recovery goals. Several are still waiting on surveys. Whatever their category, every one of the 163 will receive actions aimed at preventing its extinction. The emergen"}, {"page": "At-Risk Species", "title": "Recovery of at-risk species", "url": "species.html#recovery-of-at-risk-species", "text": "A key goal of the MNLCP is to restore at-risk species to healthy, self-sustaining populations. Some species will still need ongoing management after they reach that point. The plan calls these \"conservation-reliant\" species and considers them no longer at risk of extinction once they meet their recovery targets. The MNLCP gives managers a flexible roadmap toward recovery for all 442 at-risk species. Conservation works best when the people doing the work on the ground know what outcome they are aiming for, where the most important places are, and which actions to take first. To support that, th"}, {"page": "At-Risk Species", "title": "Three kinds of recovery goal", "url": "species.html#three-kinds-of-recovery-goal", "text": "Some at-risk species are well studied and some are barely known. So the plan sorts species into three categories, based on what is known about their biology and needs and whether proven management methods exist. Full recovery goals (211 species). These species have good information on their habitat needs, where that habitat is, their threats, and actions that work. Each has a population goal. Reaching it is expected to leave the species stable or increasing and no longer at risk, so it can come off the Maui Nui at-risk list. Many are federally and State listed and already have recovery plans o"}, {"page": "At-Risk Species", "title": "Populations: how recovery goals are measured", "url": "species.html#populations-how-recovery-goals-are-measured", "text": "Recovery goals are set as a number of healthy, self-sustaining populations. Examples from the plan: Plants: a desired number of recovery populations for each species, listed in Appendix 4. The full plant recovery footprint provides habitat for up to 10 populations of each of the 195 Plants with recovery goals. Terrestrial arthropods: an initial target of five healthy, self-sustaining populations for each species. Terrestrial snails: an interim goal of three populations for each species, listed in Appendix 6."}, {"page": "At-Risk Species", "title": "Recovery footprints", "url": "species.html#recovery-footprints", "text": "A recovery footprint is the set of planning units that contain existing or restorable habitat a group needs to reach its recovery goals. A planning unit (PU) is a mapping cell of about 2 km². Recovery footprints answer the question of \"where to focus\" conservation. Within them, priority footprints mark the most important areas to work first, and initial implementation footprints suggest where to start. Terrestrial snails are handled differently. The snail team found that all 49 at-risk snails face imminent extinction. Broad habitat recovery is not yet possible for them, so their conservation f"}, {"page": "At-Risk Species", "title": "Explore the species", "url": "species.html#explore-the-species", "text": "The species explorer below lists all 442 at-risk species in the plan. You can search by scientific, common, or Hawaiian name and filter by group, listing status, imminent extinction, and recovery category. For Plants, Terrestrial snails, Terrestrial arthropods, and Aquatic invertebrates it also shows the plan's figures for each species, such as known occupied planning units and the recovery goal. Names and figures appear as printed in the plan's appendices. Some entries are groups of related taxa or species not yet formally described, such as \" Partulina mighelsiana ssp.\" or \" Philodoria sp.\"."}, {"page": "Using the Plan", "title": "What the MNLCP provides", "url": "using-the-plan.html#what-the-mnlcp-provides", "text": "The plan gives managers three kinds of guidance. Emergency actions to prevent imminent extinctions. These are mapped where possible. Recovery actions to bring at-risk species to healthy, self-sustaining population levels. Where possible, the plan maps both the habitat each species needs and the actions to take there. Species that lack the distribution or other information needed for mapping are targeted for surveys and studies. Their results will feed updates to the MNLCP. The aim is to identify habitat needs and conservation strategies for all 442 at-risk species. Ecosystem conservation asses"}, {"page": "Using the Plan", "title": "Emergency actions to prevent extinction", "url": "using-the-plan.html#emergency-actions-to-prevent-extinction", "text": "Species experts identified emergency actions or programs to prevent imminent extinctions in Forest birds, Plants, Terrestrial snails, Terrestrial arthropods, and Aquatic invertebrates. Managing species in the wild (in situ) is usually preferred. But in situ work alone may not prevent extinction when the threat is a hard-to-detect invasive species, disease, extremely low numbers, or events that cannot be controlled, like climate change. Some emergency actions are land management in specific places. Others are research, or are not yet tied to particular sites. Each taxonomic group page gives mor"}, {"page": "Using the Plan", "title": "Where to work: habitat and spatial needs", "url": "using-the-plan.html#where-to-work-habitat-and-spatial-needs", "text": "The MNLCP recovery footprints identify the planning units (PUs, mapping cells of about 2 km²) that contain existing or restorable habitat at-risk species need to reach their recovery goals. Priority footprints pick out the most important areas to manage in the near term. Other areas follow in mid-term or later work. Combined across all eight taxonomic groups, the recovery footprint covers a significant portion of Maui Nui. It holds the habitat needed to recover, or significantly advance the recovery of, all 442 at-risk species except those still lacking distribution or habitat information. Com"}, {"page": "Using the Plan", "title": "What to do: conservation actions", "url": "using-the-plan.html#what-to-do-conservation-actions", "text": "The action prioritization effort (Priority Threat Management, or PTM, led by UH Mānoa) assessed the expected benefits of 27 conservation strategies: 17 single-focus strategies and 10 combinations of them (S18-S27). Each strategy was assessed for each species group. The analysis covered all eight MNLCP groups. It also split some groups into finer subgroups: 6 for Plants, 3 for Terrestrial arthropods, and 3 for Aquatic invertebrates."}, {"page": "Using the Plan", "title": "The goal: a 50 percent chance of persistence", "url": "using-the-plan.html#the-goal-a-50-percent-chance-of-persistence", "text": "Persistence means the probability that a species group has viable, self-sustaining populations over 20 years. For many Hawaiian species this is hard to reach. The MNLCP's target is at least a 50 percent probability of persistence for each species group. No single strategy reached 50 percent for every group. Implementing the two best-performing combination strategies together gave the best outcomes for all eight groups: S25 , a suite focused on Plants and invertebrates, and S27 , a suite focused on birds and the bat. S25 and S27 are the only combinations that include future ungulate fencing (S3"}, {"page": "Using the Plan", "title": "What the results show", "url": "using-the-plan.html#what-the-results-show", "text": "If every action in S25 and S27 were carried out and worked, every group could reach a 50 percent likelihood of persistence. Species experts judged some actions less feasible, so their expected benefits were reduced to reflect that. Overall: Taking no action leaves most species groups with a very low probability of persistence. Current actions help, but only a little for many groups. They help Seabirds and Waterbirds most. Feasible actions significantly improve persistence for each species group. Forest birds have the lowest persistence today, even with current efforts, but also the most to gai"}, {"page": "Using the Plan", "title": "When to act: sequencing", "url": "using-the-plan.html#when-to-act-sequencing", "text": "The Taxonomic Teams also looked at how best to carry out the individual actions in S25 and S27. Order matters, because some actions give more benefit than others and some must be finished before others can start. Before a key reintroduction, for example, a team may need to: identify key threats, such as diseases or ungulates, and how to control them (and develop control methods if none exist) put in fire breaks, if needed collect seeds for restoration fence the area remove ecosystem threats like ungulates remove weeds propagate the species for reintroduction reestablish and restore a native co"}, {"page": "Using the Plan", "title": "How the products fit together", "url": "using-the-plan.html#how-the-products-fit-together", "text": "Maui Nui's 442 at-risk species are not all equally imperiled. Some are down to a single individual that could die today. Others number in the thousands and may have a decade or more. Every one needs action, and the MNLCP recommends actions for all of them, but the urgency varies widely. For species not facing imminent extinction, the plan works at the planning-unit, population, and ecosystem levels to identify the habitat and actions that will advance their recovery over the life of the plan. The who, what, when, and where of conservation (plan Ch 5e, p.29) Question MNLCP product Who needs man"}, {"page": "Using the Plan", "title": "Levels of conservation footprint", "url": "using-the-plan.html#levels-of-conservation-footprint", "text": "Recovery footprints were developed by the spatial prioritization team (led by USGS) together with the Taxonomic Teams. They show the areas likely to support the number of populations needed for recovery. Within them, priority footprints flag the most important areas. For some groups, even the priority footprint is too large to manage all at once, so it was narrowed further into near-term work objectives or special actions. Each group has spatial habitat maps at these levels: Full recovery footprint: provides enough habitat for recovery. Priority recovery footprint: the most important recovery "}, {"page": "Using the Plan", "title": "Fine-scale implementation", "url": "using-the-plan.html#fine-scale-implementation", "text": "In most cases, the MNLCP maps habitat and actions at the planning-unit level. Field teams decide the exact sites within each planning unit. The MNLCP also provides implementation tools for this finer \"within PU\" planning. The Adaptive Conservation Planner (ACP) will be able to show the location of species occurrences, quality habitat, potential recovery sites, where climate seems to be changing fastest, and where certain microhabitats may be found within a planning unit. Used together with the ACP, the MNLCP is meant to be a living system. Managers can update or change it as circumstances, fun"}, {"page": "Using the Plan", "title": "How a manager would use the MNLCP", "url": "using-the-plan.html#how-a-manager-would-use-the-mnlcp", "text": "This walk-through puts the plan's guidance in order. It follows the steps Chapter 5 of the plan describes. Start with who needs help. Check the at-risk species list for the species on the lands you manage, and note which face imminent extinction. The at-risk lists answer \"who needs management\". Browse the species explorer Act on species facing imminent extinction. Use the emergency actions for each group (Table 2 above). Every species facing imminent extinction receives actions to prevent its extinction, whatever its recovery or survey category. Knowing which species face imminent extinction i"}, {"page": "Priority actions", "title": "How the actions were prioritized", "url": "actions.html#how-the-actions-were-prioritized", "text": "The Action Prioritization Team, led by UH Mānoa, used a modified Priority Threat Management (PTM) process. PTM is a structured way to compare the costs, feasibility, and benefits of management strategies and find the most cost-effective ways to raise species' chances of persistence. DOFAW and UH Mānoa convened collaborative workshops from August 2022 to March 2023. In them, roughly 100 land managers and scientists from research institutions, federal and state agencies, and non-profit organizations contributed professional judgment through open-ended discussions. Experts drew on their field exp"}, {"page": "Priority actions", "title": "The 27 strategies", "url": "actions.html#the-27-strategies", "text": "The 17 single-focus strategies run from \"no action\" (S1) to vertebrate rehabilitation (S17). The 10 combination strategies (S18-S27) mix single strategies in different ways to give a range of conservation options. The 17 single-focus strategies and their place in the two selected combination strategies. Cost is the estimated total from App 2 Table 1, in millions of dollars. <!-- src: plan Table 3 p.24, lines 1141-1162; App 2 Table 1 p.A2-15, line 5667 --> Strategy Actions Est. total cost ($M) In S25 In S27 S1 No action 0 — — S2 Existing ungulate fences 10 Yes Yes S3 Future ungulate fences 24 Y"}, {"page": "Priority actions", "title": "No single strategy is enough", "url": "actions.html#no-single-strategy-is-enough", "text": "None of the 27 strategies gives every taxonomic group a 50 percent or better chance of persistence. Implementing the two best-performing strategies together, the bird-focused S27 and the plant- and invertebrate-focused S25, gives the most beneficial outcomes for all eight taxonomic groups. S25 and S27 are the only two combinations that include future ungulate fencing (S3), terrestrial habitat management (S5), and wetland management (S6). Between them, they put all 17 single strategies to work."}, {"page": "Priority actions", "title": "Doing nothing, or only what is done now, falls short", "url": "actions.html#doing-nothing-or-only-what-is-done-now-falls-short", "text": "Taking no action gives a very low chance of persistence for most species groups. Current actions help, but the benefit is limited for many groups. Current actions help Seabirds and Waterbirds the most. Feasible new actions significantly improve the chance of persistence for every group."}, {"page": "Priority actions", "title": "Some groups are within reach; others need new tools", "url": "actions.html#some-groups-are-within-reach-others-need-new-tools", "text": "Seabirds, Waterbirds, and the Hawaiian hoary bat can reach the 50 percent goal without relying on research or unproven future methods. All other groups depend at least partly on future methods or technologies. Experts were optimistic about the bat, but there is currently no way to show numerically whether bat populations are trending up or down, so benefits to the bat cannot yet be confirmed. Forest birds have the lowest chance of persistence today, even with current efforts, but they also have the most to gain from conservation action. Avian malaria is their most important threat. Tools that "}, {"page": "Priority actions", "title": "New tools are the single most important investment", "url": "actions.html#new-tools-are-the-single-most-important-investment", "text": "Future tools for rare plant and invertebrate conservation (S13) is the single most important individual strategy. Thirteen of the 17 species groups ranked it in their top three. Only the three bird groups and the bat did not. S13 ranks this high because it: increases facilities for captive rearing and propagation of plants and invertebrates; develops methods for cryopreservation and tissue culture; and develops landscape-scale control for threats that are now hard or impossible to manage, including predatory snails, slugs, black twig borer, coconut rhinoceros beetle, naio thrips, ants, wasps, "}, {"page": "Priority actions", "title": "Foundational habitat work pays off across groups", "url": "actions.html#foundational-habitat-work-pays-off-across-groups", "text": "Management actions such as invasive predator control, ungulate control, and habitat management are important parts of the strategies with the greatest benefits across species. Landscape-scale actions give some benefit to every group. Preventing the extinction of every group, however, also takes species-specific actions, such as predator exclusion fences for seabirds, waterbirds, and snails. Expected chance of persistence (percent, over 20 years, adjusted for feasibility) for each species group under no action, current management, S25 and S27. Bold values meet the 50 percent target. <!-- src: p"}, {"page": "Priority actions", "title": "What it costs", "url": "actions.html#what-it-costs", "text": "Preventing the extinction of all species groups over the next 20 years will cost about $55 million per year on average. Much of the cost falls in the first year: an upfront investment of about $67 million , mostly for building fences and facilities. This covers new field vehicles, propagation and rehabilitation facilities, research and development, and new ungulate and predator exclusion fences. Fence construction continues in years 2-10, and vehicles are replaced in years 11 and 16. About $53 million per year maintains fences and facilities and carries out landscape-scale actions. Most recurr"}, {"page": "Priority actions", "title": "Combined priority actions across groups", "url": "actions.html#combined-priority-actions-across-groups", "text": "Because S25 and S27 together include every single-focus strategy, the MNLCP's action program is the full set of 17 strategies. S25 is the more plant- and invertebrate-oriented suite of actions. S27 is more focused on birds and the bat. The Taxonomic Teams also decided how best to sequence the individual actions within S25 and S27 for each group. Order matters for two reasons: some actions give more benefit than others, and some must be finished before others can start. Before reintroducing a plant species, for example, managers may need to: identify key threats, such as disease or ungulates, a"}, {"page": "Priority actions", "title": "Emergency actions to prevent extinction", "url": "actions.html#emergency-actions-to-prevent-extinction", "text": "Some species cannot wait. Of the 442 at-risk species, 163 face imminent extinction within five years without emergency action. Species experts identified emergency actions or programs for Forest birds, Plants, Terrestrial snails, Terrestrial arthropods, and Aquatic invertebrates. In situ management is usually preferred, but it may not be enough when the threat is a cryptic invasive species, disease, extremely low numbers, or events that cannot be controlled, such as climate change. Species facing imminent extinction and the emergency actions needed now <!-- src: plan Table 2 p.21, lines 1040-1"}, {"page": "Priority actions", "title": "Strategy explorer", "url": "actions.html#strategy-explorer", "text": "Choose a strategy to see its actions, its estimated cost, and the expected chance of persistence for each of the 17 species groups. Groups that reach the 50 percent target are highlighted. Compare any two strategies side by side. Methods digest: Priority Threat Management (Appendix 2) Details Scope. The PTM analysis covered 374 species at risk of extinction within 20 years, in 17 species groups: Forest birds, Seabirds, Waterbirds, six plant groups, Terrestrial snails, yellow-faced bees, picture-winged flies, Blackburn's sphinx moth, damselflies, anchialine pool shrimp, freshwater snails, and t"}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "Why ecosystems matter", "url": "ecosystems.html#why-ecosystems-matter", "text": "Conserving native ecosystems helps prevent more plants and animals from declining to the point of being at risk of extinction. Protected and restored ecosystems also help Maui Nui's 442 at-risk species recover and stay recovered. They provide connectivity, buffers from threats, redundancy, and room for populations to grow. The MNLCP's ecosystem work looks for three things: remaining good-quality native ecosystems and unique communities, the conservation strategies that will most help reach ecosystem goals, and conservation opportunities based on habitat quality, the conservation status of land"}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "The terrestrial ecosystems of Maui Nui", "url": "ecosystems.html#the-terrestrial-ecosystems-of-maui-nui", "text": "The islands of Maui Nui (Hawaiian for \"Big Maui\") are Molokaʻi, Lānaʻi, Maui, and Kahoʻolawe. Together they span almost the whole range of environments found across the Hawaiian Islands, from tropical coastlines and young lava flows to rainforests and an alpine summit. For most of their history these four islands were joined as one large island, Maui Nui, which at its largest was bigger than today's Hawaiʻi Island. As the land sank, the connections between the islands went under the sea. During the low sea levels of glacial periods the islands reunited, as recently as 20,000 years ago. Because"}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "How ecosystems were delineated", "url": "ecosystems.html#how-ecosystems-were-delineated", "text": "The plan maps ten ecosystems. They start from the moisture zones mapped by Price et al. (2012), which are then split into four elevation bands using a USGS digital elevation model: Lowland: 0 to 1,000 m Montane: 1,000 to 2,000 m Subalpine: 2,000 to 3,000 m Alpine: above 3,000 m The Wet and Mesic zones reach only slightly into the Subalpine band, so those areas are folded into Montane Wet and Montane Mesic. That gives nine ecosystems defined by climate. The tenth, Lava Pioneer , is kept separate because its young lava substrates create unique growing conditions. Map of the ten major ecosystems "}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "Special ecological features", "url": "ecosystems.html#special-ecological-features", "text": "Some important habitats are too small or too patchy to map at the landscape scale. The plan recognizes five of these special features, each supporting unique plants and animals: Coastal areas: a varied mix of cliffs, sand dunes and wetlands. Anchialine pools: landlocked pools linked to the ocean underground through porous volcanic rock. They occur in only a few places in the world and hold unique animals. Lava tubes: caves formed by recent volcanic activity, home to unique animals. Cliffs: long a refuge for unique Hawaiian plants. Recent exploration with drones has found species that had never"}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "Ecosystems at a glance", "url": "ecosystems.html#ecosystems-at-a-glance", "text": "Lowland Arid. The most reduced and degraded ecosystem. It is poorly mapped and very little habitat is left. Restoration, prioritized within the recovery footprint, is the main opportunity. Lowland Dry. Once the most extensive ecosystem, it is now down to about two percent of its original area. It is a hotspot for rare species, and restoration is the key opportunity. Better high-resolution mapping is needed to find remaining pockets of native vegetation. Lowland Mesic. A fragmented ecosystem. Areas mapped as \"native dominated\" often have a heavy understory of invasive plants, so \"Restore\" descr"}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "Habitat quality", "url": "ecosystems.html#habitat-quality", "text": "Hawaiian vegetation ranges from mostly native to entirely dominated by invasive species. Using vegetation cover classes from Jacobi et al. (2017), the plan sorts land into three habitat quality zones: High: dominated by native plants Low: dominated by non-native plants Developed or cropland The source map did not classify \"sparsely vegetated\" areas. Those inside the Lava Pioneer ecosystem were counted as High quality and those elsewhere as Low, based on vegetation surveys. Map of remaining habitat quality across Maui Nui ecosystems. Plan Figure 22."}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "The management landscape", "url": "ecosystems.html#the-management-landscape", "text": "Feral ungulates are a primary cause of damage to Hawaiian ecosystems, and invasive species mean that simply protecting land from development is not enough. Land needs active management. Building on the earlier Hawaiʻi Gap Analysis Program (HIGAP), the plan sorts land into three tiers of management potential: Ongoing ungulate exclusion. Areas where feral ungulates have been kept out by fencing and removal. These have the highest management potential. They are freed from ungulate damage, and other conservation work, such as planting endangered plants, is more likely to succeed there. Conservatio"}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "Four kinds of management opportunity", "url": "ecosystems.html#four-kinds-of-management-opportunity", "text": "Overlaying habitat quality on management potential shows what kind of opportunity each area offers. Management opportunities by habitat quality and management potential (plan Tables 29 and 31) Management potential Low-quality habitat Medium-quality habitat High-quality habitat Managed (ungulates excluded) Unlikely combination (roads and infrastructure inside managed areas) Restore within ungulate-excluded areas 1. Maintain: managed high-quality habitat Unmanaged, partner lands Management or restoration unlikely (agricultural areas) Restore within the recovery footprint 2. Initiate: management "}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "Biodiversity hotspots", "url": "ecosystems.html#biodiversity-hotspots", "text": "To add context, the plan mapped overall plant diversity. Each of 1,087 plant species' range maps (Price et al. 2012) was overlaid, and the number of species was counted in every 30 m grid cell. A second map repeated this for the quarter of species that have lost the most habitat, measured as the share of their original habitat no longer in high-quality condition. Total diversity reflects climate, island history and the age of the land. Species with the greatest habitat loss are concentrated in the ecosystems most disrupted by people. Two maps of plant species richness, (a) all Maui Nui plant s"}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "Landscape patterns of management opportunity", "url": "ecosystems.html#landscape-patterns-of-management-opportunity", "text": "Combining these layers shows a landscape pattern of management opportunities that differs by ecosystem. The MNLCP's long-term goal is to restore all ecosystems to a level that supports all of Maui Nui's terrestrial and freshwater biodiversity. Some ecosystems have less than one percent of their original area left as high-quality native habitat. The pattern is clear in the numbers. In the upper elevations, most of what remains is already managed: three quarters of Montane Wet and almost two thirds of Subalpine fall in the \"Maintain\" category. In the lowlands, the largest opportunities are resto"}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "Overall priorities for terrestrial ecosystems", "url": "ecosystems.html#overall-priorities-for-terrestrial-ecosystems", "text": "Setting priorities among ecosystems is hard, but the plan gives three general guidelines: Protect what is still good first. Protecting and managing existing high-quality habitat generally comes before restoring degraded areas. This matters most in ecosystems with less than 40 percent of their original area left as high-quality habitat. Start where management is already possible. Managing high-quality areas already in conservation-oriented status can usually be done faster than elsewhere. Restore where it counts. Restoring areas that matter for species recovery, connectivity, high species diver"}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "Climate change patterns", "url": "ecosystems.html#climate-change-patterns", "text": "Climate change is an ongoing threat even inside high-quality native ecosystems. Overlaying projected climate change on the map of high-quality habitat shows that most of this habitat is changing more slowly than the Maui Nui average, but it is still changing. Managers should track these changes and decide whether management needs to adjust. Most remaining high-quality native habitat is changing more slowly than the rest of Maui Nui under climate change, but it is still changing, and management will need to keep pace. Overlay of climate change vectors on high-quality native habitat. Plan Figure"}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "Priorities for managing key landscape areas", "url": "ecosystems.html#priorities-for-managing-key-landscape-areas", "text": "Maintain Managed (ungulate exclusion) + high-quality habitat Maintain managed high-quality habitat that has ungulate fences. Existing management should continue. Initiate Unmanaged partner lands in conservation status + high-quality habitat Initiate management on lands in conservation status: identify existing native ecosystems that are in conservation status but not yet fenced. Maui Nui has extensive areas in conservation status that are not protected from ungulate damage, and some are important for both ecosystems and at-risk species recovery. Key areas: Windward Haleakalā (East Maui); Molok"}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "Priority 1: Maintain", "url": "ecosystems.html#priority-1-maintain", "text": "Keep managing high-quality habitat that is already fenced against ungulates. Existing management should continue. This is the highest-quality habitat that is already protected, and keeping it intact comes first."}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "Priority 2: Initiate", "url": "ecosystems.html#priority-2-initiate", "text": "Start management on high-quality habitat in lands already in conservation status but not yet fenced. Maui Nui has large areas in conservation status that are not protected from ungulate damage. Some are important for both ecosystems and at-risk species recovery. The plan highlights five numbered areas, including windward Haleakalā, two areas on Molokaʻi, and Lānaʻihale. Opportunity areas for ungulate control (areas 1 to 5): high-quality native habitat shown by conservation status and fencing. Plan Figure 27."}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "Priority 3: Engage", "url": "ecosystems.html#priority-3-engage", "text": "Work with potential partners to manage high-quality habitat or bring it into conservation status. Key areas for engagement include eastern Molokaʻi, the slopes above Makawao on windward Haleakalā, and the Kahikinui area. Opportunity areas for engagement with land managers (areas 6 to 8). Plan Figure 28."}, {"page": "Ecosystems", "title": "Priority 4: Restore", "url": "ecosystems.html#priority-4-restore", "text": "Restore medium-quality habitat to help meet ecosystem area targets and other values, especially recovery of at-risk species. Restoration focuses on medium-quality habitat inside ungulate-excluded areas or within the recovery footprint. Areas that support species recovery, connectivity, high diversity or climate resilience come before areas that only add acres. Methods in brief Details Ecosystem map. Moisture zones from Price et al. (2012) split by elevation (USGS digital elevation model) into Lowland, Montane, Subalpine and Alpine bands. Wet and Mesic subalpine areas merged into Montane Wet an"}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Why monitor", "url": "monitoring.html#why-monitor", "text": "Recovering at-risk species and restoring native ecosystems is very complex. The MNLCP identifies priority species and ecosystems, the populations most in need of emergency action, the threats that most affect each one, and the management actions expected to move them toward recovery. But managers also need to know whether those actions are producing a positive ecological response, and to be able to respond when results call for a change in strategy or methods. Uncertainty is part of conservation work, and monitoring is how managers learn. Monitoring has shown, for example, that: mosquito contr"}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "What adaptive management is", "url": "monitoring.html#what-adaptive-management-is", "text": "Adaptive management is a structured, step-by-step learning process that reduces uncertainty and leads, over time, to more effective actions. It has three parts: collecting monitoring data, evaluating that data, and using the evaluation to guide management decisions. Monitoring alone is not enough. The data must be evaluated and shared to influence decisions. Adaptive management lets recovery actions go ahead despite uncertainty. It uses what is known and what is learned to confirm or revise strategies. And it leads to more effective, efficient and lasting results by prompting changes when an a"}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "What a good program delivers", "url": "monitoring.html#what-a-good-program-delivers", "text": "A well-run adaptive management program: collects data over the long time frames needed to detect real change; produces tangible evidence of progress that supports institutional and funding decisions; allows managers to assess return on investment; improves understanding of the effects of management and of how species and ecosystems behave; and sets benchmarks that staff and partners can recognize as success. It is also demanding. It needs sustained commitment, leadership, funding and partner involvement. Monitoring must focus on realistic objectives, be built into management, and be paired wit"}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "How the MNLCP frames monitoring", "url": "monitoring.html#how-the-mnlcp-frames-monitoring", "text": "The MNLCP addresses 442 at-risk species across eight taxonomic groups, with management actions prioritized across Maui Nui. For each species it sets quantitative population targets in a common currency of distribution and abundance and assigns them to specific planning units (PUs), the roughly 2 km² building blocks of the plan's maps. These targets say not just how many individuals are needed for recovery, but where they are needed. The Adaptive Conservation Planner makes the targets available to managers for each species and PU. So whatever method is used, the core question is the same: is th"}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "The adaptive management cycle", "url": "monitoring.html#the-adaptive-management-cycle", "text": "Adaptive management repeats as a cycle. Each turn starts from what was learned in the last one. Set goals and objectives. Define the desired outcome and a monitoring objective for a species or ecosystem in a specific place. The MNLCP already provides the priority species or ecosystem, its location, and its PU targets. Design the monitoring. Choose what to measure, how much effort is warranted, the monitoring design, and a written protocol. Act and monitor. Carry out management and collect monitoring data, using the same methods at the same locations over time. Manage and analyze the data. Chec"}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Key adaptive management terms", "url": "monitoring.html#key-adaptive-management-terms", "text": "Monitoring is gathering information through repeated measurements over time, and comparable data from multiple samples, to detect change, interpret results and improve management decisions. It is both a process (repeated measurement) and a purpose (better decisions). It tracks the status of populations and ecosystems against a desired condition, how well management works, how strong and damaging threats are, and how efficient management and monitoring are, and it helps spot new threats. Because populations take years to expand and ecosystems take decades to recover, monitoring takes time to pa"}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Five types of monitoring objectives", "url": "monitoring.html#five-types-of-monitoring-objectives", "text": "Monitoring can focus on one or more of five objectives."}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Implementation monitoring", "url": "monitoring.html#implementation-monitoring", "text": "Question: Was the planned action done, and how? It checks that a management action was completed and records where, when, how and by whom."}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Effectiveness monitoring", "url": "monitoring.html#effectiveness-monitoring", "text": "Question: Did the action improve conditions for the population or ecosystem? For example: fewer threats, more individuals, higher survival and reproduction, healthier individuals, or a restored native understory."}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Outcome monitoring", "url": "monitoring.html#outcome-monitoring", "text": "Question: Is a suite of actions preventing extinction and advancing recovery? This longer-term view combines data across all targeted populations of a species or ecosystem and adds signs of self-sustaining populations, such as new plants establishing, a growing range, fledglings produced, and recruitment of key ecosystem species."}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Impact monitoring", "url": "monitoring.html#impact-monitoring", "text": "Question: Is the program as a whole making progress toward recovery and restoration? It is a program-wide assessment, often summarized in a clear, well-designed dashboard."}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Validation monitoring", "url": "monitoring.html#validation-monitoring", "text": "Question: Which management methods work best, and why? Closer to research, it compares methods to find the most effective and efficient ones and explores the causes behind management responses. A comprehensive adaptive management program should include the first four types. Validation monitoring is used where there is a specific need to better understand a management action."}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Developing a monitoring plan", "url": "monitoring.html#developing-a-monitoring-plan", "text": "The plan describes five key steps. It gives enough detail to show what information is needed. Fully developing a monitoring plan will need more guidance from qualified experts."}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Step 1. Specify the management goal and monitoring objectives", "url": "monitoring.html#step-1-specify-the-management-goal-and-monitoring-objectives", "text": "A management goal describes the desired outcome over a set time: what actions will be taken and the hoped-for impact. That impact can be a direction of change (more individuals, less invasive cover) or a threshold (raise a population above 400, or cut invasive cover below 10 percent). A monitoring objective has five parts: the species or ecosystem, the location, the attributes to measure, the method, and the time frame for management and monitoring. This step also covers telling the target apart from similar species or ecosystems, and detectability . Detection is rarely perfect: in some cases "}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Step 2. Select attributes to measure", "url": "monitoring.html#step-2-select-attributes-to-measure", "text": "Attributes (also called indicators, metrics or parameters) are the characteristics measured to track change. For species: number of individuals, condition (survival, health, vigor, damage) and reproduction. Long-lived species may change slowly in abundance but faster in reproduction. For ecosystems: composition, vertical and horizontal structure, connectivity, species richness, reproduction of key species such as canopy or pollinator plants, and processes such as light-gap formation or fire and rain events. Indirect indicators can help when the link is known or reasonably assumed: canopy openn"}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Step 3. Decide how much to invest in monitoring", "url": "monitoring.html#step-3-decide-how-much-to-invest-in-monitoring", "text": "With 442 species, from invertebrates to birds, across 492 planning units, few projects can support a fully robust statistical design. But all can give useful insight if effort is focused well. The plan's criteria for setting the level of monitoring are: Vulnerability , such as probability of extinction. For plants, this is reflected in PEPP (Plant Extinction Prevention Program) status. Among forest birds, kiwikiu and ʻākohekohe are considered most vulnerable. Uncertainty in the outcome of an action. Greater uncertainty calls for more monitoring; proven approaches such as fencing and ungulate c"}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Step 4. Develop a monitoring design", "url": "monitoring.html#step-4-develop-a-monitoring-design", "text": "The design sets how data will be collected and is central to precise, reliable and defensible results. There are three general approaches: Complete census. Count every individual (or unit, such as burrows), or fully measure an ecosystem, within a defined area. Quick, easy to evaluate and explain, and needs no statistics, which makes it efficient for many at-risk species with small populations. Index sample. Track trends in one or more sample areas that are not randomly placed. It works well when experienced ecologists choose the locations, but results cannot be used to estimate the whole popul"}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Step 5. Compile a monitoring protocol", "url": "monitoring.html#step-5-compile-a-monitoring-protocol", "text": "A monitoring protocol is a living document, ideally online and updated every year, that explains how monitoring is done: purpose, design, methods, data management and analysis, evaluation, reporting, and roles. Its main value is keeping monitoring consistent over time, across sites and among staff. Best practice includes standard operating procedures and testing the protocol with someone unfamiliar with the site. Full checklist: steps to initiate a monitoring plan Details The plan's sidebar lists ten steps: Specify the management goal and monitoring objective, and confirm the target can be rel"}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "From monitoring data to adaptive decisions", "url": "monitoring.html#from-monitoring-data-to-adaptive-decisions", "text": "Monitoring data are valuable only when they are available for analysis, evaluation and decisions, over both short and long time frames."}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Managing and analyzing data", "url": "monitoring.html#managing-and-analyzing-data", "text": "Detecting change usually takes data from many sampling cycles, so long-term datasets must be kept intact. That means clear procedures for collecting data, metadata, an accessible database, quality checks, infrastructure for future analysis, and lossless, nonproprietary file formats with clear archiving practices. Data quality is assured in three steps: verification (is it complete?), validation (does it make scientific sense?) and certification (is it ready to use?). For census and index data, results can usually be plotted and reviewed across years, keeping in mind what the data do and do not"}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Evaluation and reporting", "url": "monitoring.html#evaluation-and-reporting", "text": "Evaluation is the systematic, objective review of monitoring data to decide whether actions are working. It happens annually for effectiveness and every five to ten years for recovery status. It looks at the type and implementation of actions, the population or habitat response, emerging threats, and how to allocate resources. Evaluation should happen soon after data are available, while field details are fresh. With Hawaiʻi's varied growing and breeding seasons, that may mean quarterly evaluation meetings. Good evaluation is structured (a facilitated process covering all relevant information)"}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "Communicating results", "url": "monitoring.html#communicating-results", "text": "Managers and decision-makers are the main audience. They need clear recommendations backed by data so they can report progress to administrators and funders. Other audiences include conservation practitioners, political leaders, institutions and the public. A communication plan can match each audience with key messages and channels, from written materials to social media, web content, video and presentations. Two products have worked well elsewhere: An annual accomplishment report summarizing management, monitoring and protection by taxonomic group, species and area, with measures such as mile"}, {"page": "Monitoring", "title": "A programmatic path forward", "url": "monitoring.html#a-programmatic-path-forward", "text": "The MNLCP, the Adaptive Conservation Planner, and DOFAW's commitment to protecting biodiversity create a unique opportunity to build a comprehensive, lasting adaptive management program. It will need to be tied to the missions of DOFAW and partner agencies, cover species, taxonomic groups and ecosystems, and continue over the long term. The plan names seven critical components, adapted from Kusek and Rist (2004): Institutional understanding: information and training so staff, agencies and partners understand and support the program. A continuous need for monitoring data: program requirements t"}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "What the Adaptive Conservation Planner is", "url": "tools.html#what-the-adaptive-conservation-planner-is", "text": "The Adaptive Conservation Planner (ACP, or \"Conservation Planner\" for short) is where the MNLCP's final spatial products live. It holds the recovery footprints for each taxonomic group, the planning units (PUs) that make them up, the species targeted in each unit, and supporting information on species, habitats, and threats. It is part of the TFE-SCP process, the comprehensive planning process used for the plan's spatial prioritization. The ACP carries conservation footprints from draft prioritization results, through fine-tuning by species experts, and into long-term care of the plan. A conse"}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "Who it is for", "url": "tools.html#who-it-is-for", "text": "The ACP is a working tool for the people who carry out the plan: DOFAW staff, taxon experts, and land managers. It is not a public web map and it is not a task tracker. Users sign in with an account, and what each person can do depends on their role: Read-only users can view footprints, map layers, and species targets. Users can also propose footprint edits and write notes. Admins review and approve proposed edits and manage accounts. Sensitive information, such as exact locations of rare species, is shown only to accounts that have been given permission for it."}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "Plan the year's work", "url": "tools.html#plan-the-year-s-work", "text": "The plan describes the ACP as the tool managers use to develop annual work plans, adjust strategies based on results and new information, and explore different management scenarios. For example, managers can narrow a priority footprint to the species in greatest need, such as plants facing imminent extinction that are known from only one or a few planning units, to set a focused first round of work. See Using the Plan for the full worked example."}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "View footprints by group or species", "url": "tools.html#view-footprints-by-group-or-species", "text": "Choose one or more taxonomic groups and the ACP draws their conservation footprints on the map, separately or overlaid. With several groups loaded, you can show a combined footprint, or a target richness map that colors each planning unit by how many at-risk species are targeted there. The richness view helps find exceptional areas that support many at-risk species or species facing imminent extinction. Search for a single species to see its suitable habitat, its recovery and priority units, and its known populations."}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "See what is targeted in each planning unit", "url": "tools.html#see-what-is-targeted-in-each-planning-unit", "text": "Select a planning unit to see which species are targeted there, their habitat suitability, the evidence that they occur there, and whether their targets are met."}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "Add context layers", "url": "tools.html#add-context-layers", "text": "Overlay other maps, such as biodiversity reserves, fire risk, or climate resilience, to see how a footprint relates to land status and threats. Users can upload their own layers as well."}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "Propose changes and add notes", "url": "tools.html#propose-changes-and-add-notes", "text": "Authorized users can propose edits: move a species target to a different planning unit, add a new target location, remove a target, or change whether a target is part of the priority footprint. They can also attach notes to species or planning units to record field observations, access concerns, land-tenure information, or revisions needed later."}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "Export the data", "url": "tools.html#export-the-data", "text": "Download footprint data as CSV, GeoPackage, or GeoJSON for use in GIS or other planning systems. Exports can be limited to selected groups, species, or planning units."}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "How the ACP keeps the plan current", "url": "tools.html#how-the-acp-keeps-the-plan-current", "text": "The ACP keeps two things side by side: A versioned official footprint: a stable, dated snapshot (for example, the 2026 official footprint) used for reporting, hand-off, and as the formal reference for implementation. A living platform: where ongoing edits, annual reviews, newly found populations, and changes in management conditions are added, with a record of how and why each change was made. This means DOFAW can give partners a stable product to plan around while keeping the footprints up to date as knowledge improves. Future annual reviews can change specific planning units, and the change "}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "Screenshot walkthrough", "url": "tools.html#screenshot-walkthrough", "text": "The images below follow a first session in the Conservation Planner. 1. The main screen. Map Layers, Targets, and Edits tabs on the left; the map on the right. Here Plants, Terrestrial snails, and Terrestrial arthropods are loaded and the target richness map shows where targets cluster. 2. Choose taxonomic groups. The selector at the top loads one or more groups' footprints; each loaded group then appears as a layer you can switch on or off. 3. The layout with one group loaded. The Terrestrial snails footprint is shown in blue; hovering over a planning unit shows its ID. 4. Map Layers. Toggle "}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "Getting access", "url": "tools.html#getting-access", "text": "The plan lists the following address for the Conservation Planner: Open the Adaptive Conservation Planner An account is needed to sign in. Accounts are set up by an ACP administrator, who assigns each person a role. [PLACEHOLDER: how partners request an account, and whom to contact. Not stated in the plan; DOFAW to supply.] The ACP will be described in more detail in the Managers' Handbook being developed for DOFAW staff."}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "Current status", "url": "tools.html#current-status", "text": "The Conservation Planner is in operational use. It has been used to fine-tune the plant recovery footprint (its most mature use), to review snail exclosure (kīpuka kāhuli) sites, and to review arthropod targets. It handles footprints for several taxonomic groups at once and has been tested through full edit, review, and approval cycles. Planned improvements include: - better display of occurrence data for some groups - automatic detection of conflicting edits between recovery and priority footprints - custom overlays for individual species - clearer save and publish behavior - documentation so"}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "Other tools", "url": "tools.html#other-tools", "text": "The plan lists four companion tools. Only Find My Microhabitat is available now; the others will be released in the near future."}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "Find My Microhabitat (available)", "url": "tools.html#find-my-microhabitat-available", "text": "Conservation footprints answer the question \"where should we focus?\" at the scale of planning units, which can be hundreds of hectares. But an outplanting site, survey transect, or exclosure covers a fraction of a hectare. Find My Microhabitat bridges that gap: within a selected planning unit, it combines high-resolution data on vegetation structure, canopy cover, topographic position, moisture, and other local habitat variables to find the most suitable microsites for target species. It is useful for: - Outplanting: finding the spots within a footprint planning unit where rare plant reintrodu"}, {"page": "Tools", "title": "Coming soon", "url": "tools.html#coming-soon", "text": "Potential Recovery Sites (PRS): map layers of potential recovery sites based on climate suitability and habitat quality, to help managers focus field searches for suitable sites. Climate Vectors Footprint Summarizer The plan says these within-unit tools will let the Conservation Planner show managers where species occur, where habitat quality is high, where potential recovery sites are, where climatic conditions seem to be changing most rapidly, and where certain microhabitats might be found within a planning unit."}, {"page": "Resources", "title": "Download the plan", "url": "resources.html#download-the-plan", "text": "Download the Maui Nui Landscape Conservation Plan (PDF) Maui Nui Landscape Conservation Plan: Maui, Molokaʻi, Lānaʻi, and Kahoʻolawe. Hawaiʻi Division of Forestry and Wildlife. Version September 29, 2026. The plan is a living document. In its own words, it \"is subject to periodic review and revision.\" Check back for updated versions. The plan covers the 442 native species at risk of extinction in Maui Nui, where to recover them, which actions will do the most good, and how to track progress over a 20-year implementation period."}, {"page": "Resources", "title": "Conservation footprint map file (KMZ)", "url": "resources.html#conservation-footprint-map-file-kmz", "text": "A Google Earth (KMZ) file of the conservation footprints will be offered here once DOFAW approves its release. The file will show the recovery footprints for each taxonomic group and open in Google Earth and most GIS programs. To explore the footprints online, use the Conservation Footprints map . Footprints are living products and are updated in the Adaptive Conservation Planner . The file will be a dated snapshot; the official, current footprints are kept in the Conservation Planner. Partners with an ACP account can export current footprint data as CSV, GeoPackage, or GeoJSON."}, {"page": "Resources", "title": "How to cite the plan", "url": "resources.html#how-to-cite-the-plan", "text": "The plan does not give a recommended citation. Based on its title page, we suggest: Hawaiʻi Division of Forestry and Wildlife (DOFAW). 2026. Maui Nui Landscape Conservation Plan: Maui, Molokaʻi, Lānaʻi, and Kahoʻolawe. Version September 29, 2026. Honolulu: Hawaiʻi Department of Land and Natural Resources, Division of Forestry and Wildlife. The place of publication follows the form the plan uses for another DOFAW publication in its Literature Cited."}, {"page": "Resources", "title": "Contact", "url": "resources.html#contact", "text": "[PLACEHOLDER: DOFAW contact for questions about the plan and for Adaptive Conservation Planner access (name or office, email). The plan does not name a contact; DOFAW to supply.]"}, {"page": "Resources", "title": "Related plans and guidance", "url": "resources.html#related-plans-and-guidance", "text": "The MNLCP does not replace existing plans. Hawaiʻi already has many high-quality plans, but they are not integrated at the landscape or management level, and managers must reconcile dozens of documents with different assumptions and scales. The MNLCP brings them together into one landscape-scale strategy. The plan builds on: Species recovery plans. Each at-risk species' recovery target is based on abundance, distribution, and viability criteria derived from existing recovery plans where they exist, supplemented by expert input. The Hawaiʻi State Wildlife Action Plan (SWAP). One of the sources "}, {"page": "Resources", "title": "Coming next", "url": "resources.html#coming-next", "text": "Managers' Handbook. A practical guide for DOFAW staff using MNLCP tools and outputs, being developed under the Implementation Team."}, {"page": "Resources", "title": "Literature cited", "url": "resources.html#literature-cited", "text": "The full list of works cited in the plan, as printed in its Literature Cited section. Allison, Allen. 2003. “Biological Surveys—New Perspectives in the Pacific.” Organisms Diversity and Evolution 3: 103–10. Camaclang, A. E., J. Currie, E. Giles, et al. 2021. “Prioritizing Threat Management across Terrestrial and Freshwater Realms for Species Conservation and Recovery.” Conservation Science and Practice 3: e300. Carwardine, Josie, Tara G. Martin, Jennifer Firn, et al. 2019. “Priority Threat Management for Biodiversity Conservation: A Handbook.” Journal of Applied Ecology 56: 481–90. Cowie, Robe"}, {"page": "Resources", "title": "Photo credits", "url": "resources.html#photo-credits", "text": "Photographs and illustrations used on this site, with their sources and licences."}, {"page": "Resources", "title": "Glossary", "url": "resources.html#glossary", "text": "Definitions of terms used in the plan, such as planning unit, recovery footprint, and priority footprint, are on the Glossary page."}, {"page": "Forest birds", "title": "Why forest birds matter here", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html#why-forest-birds-matter-here", "text": "Forest birds had the lowest chance of persisting of any group in the plan, both with no action and with current actions. They also had the most to gain from new conservation action. If all actions are implemented and effective, forest birds reach the highest persistence of any group (71.6 percent). Development and use of effective controls for mosquito-borne avian malaria is \"clearly the most important action\" for Maui Nui's forest birds."}, {"page": "Forest birds", "title": "The species", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html#the-species", "text": "At-risk forest birds in the MNLCP Hawaiian name Common name Scientific name Status Faces imminent extinction Where it lives now Kiwikiu Maui parrotbill Pseudonestor xanthophrys Endangered (State and federal) Yes About 30 km² of East Maui rainforest above 1,500 m ʻĀkohekohe Crested honeycreeper Palmeria dolei Endangered (State and federal) Yes A small area of mesic and montane forest on Haleakalā, East Maui ʻIʻiwi (none given) Drepanis coccinea Federally threatened; State endangered on Oʻahu, Molokaʻi and Lānaʻi No Mostly above 1,250 m; in Maui Nui, all on East Maui Maui ʻalauahio Maui creeper "}, {"page": "Forest birds", "title": "Kiwikiu (Maui parrotbill)", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html#kiwikiu-maui-parrotbill", "text": "An insectivorous bird once found on Maui and Molokaʻi. It now lives in about 30 km² of native-dominated rainforest above 1,500 m on East Maui, with fewer than 150 birds. Two non-reproductive birds are in captivity. The plan describes the species as at very high risk of extinction in the near future."}, {"page": "Forest birds", "title": "ʻĀkohekohe (crested honeycreeper)", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html#ʻākohekohe-crested-honeycreeper", "text": "Once found on Molokaʻi and Maui, the ʻākohekohe now occupies a small area of mesic and montane forest on Haleakalā. The wild population is estimated at 1,200 to 2,000 birds, with none in captivity. It is at high risk of extinction in the near future."}, {"page": "Forest birds", "title": "ʻIʻiwi", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html#ʻiʻiwi", "text": "Once common at lower elevations throughout the main Hawaiian Islands, ʻiʻiwi now occur mainly above 1,250 m. About 50,000 live in Maui Nui, all on East Maui."}, {"page": "Forest birds", "title": "Maui ʻalauahio (Maui creeper)", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html#maui-ʻalauahio-maui-creeper", "text": "Not listed under State or federal law, but declining. The Forest Bird Team included it because of steep recent declines in many other Maui forest birds. The population is estimated at 99,000, all on East Maui. Why the ʻalalā is not in this plan Details The ʻalalā ( Corvus hawaiiensis , Hawaiian crow) is native to Hawaiʻi Island and has recently been introduced to Maui. The plan does not address it because the introduction of up to 15 crows is a five-year pilot. The pilot will test whether ʻalalā can establish where there are no native avian predators such as the ʻio ( Buteo solitarius , Hawaii"}, {"page": "Forest birds", "title": "Status and trends", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html#status-and-trends", "text": "After years of fairly stable numbers, both kiwikiu and ʻākohekohe have recently declined sharply, raising concern for their survival. The plan also notes a \"recent precipitous decline\" in many other Maui forest bird species. Every at-risk forest bird in Maui Nui is now found only on East Maui. Estimated current populations (individuals) Species East Maui West Maui Molokaʻi Lānaʻi Kahoʻolawe Total Kiwikiu 150 0 0 0 0 150 ʻĀkohekohe 1,768 0 0 0 0 1,768 ʻIʻiwi 50,000 0 0 0 0 50,000 Maui ʻalauahio 99,000 0 0 0 0 99,000"}, {"page": "Forest birds", "title": "Threats", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html#threats", "text": "Avian malaria. Introduced avian malaria, carried by introduced Culex mosquitoes, is the primary threat to kiwikiu and ʻākohekohe and the main driver of forest bird decline. Warming climate. Continued warming is likely to let Culex mosquitoes spread further upslope and further threaten forest birds (Fortini et al. 2015). Predators and habitat loss. Invasive vertebrate predators and degraded habitat are also addressed by the plan's action strategies (vertebrate predator control, rat suppression, habitat management and ungulate fencing). The plan describes forest birds, like Terrestrial snails, a"}, {"page": "Forest birds", "title": "Emergency actions to prevent extinction", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html#emergency-actions-to-prevent-extinction", "text": "The Forest Bird Team identified kiwikiu and ʻākohekohe as at very high risk of near-term extinction. The Maui Forest Bird Recovery Project identified the priority actions to prevent their extinction. Kiwikiu: expand the habitat birds can use by reducing mosquitoes and disease transmission in three areas in and next to currently occupied habitat. ʻĀkohekohe: reduce mosquitoes and disease in the same three areas, and establish a second population by translocating birds to suitable habitat not on Maui. Map of East Maui showing current endangered forest bird range and the three priority mosquito-c"}, {"page": "Forest birds", "title": "Goals", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html#goals", "text": "Recovery for all four species means three primary populations, one each on East Maui, West Maui and Molokaʻi. Kiwikiu and ʻākohekohe have targets for each area. For ʻiʻiwi and Maui ʻalauahio, the target is a total across all three areas. Recovery population targets (individuals) Species East Maui West Maui Molokaʻi Lānaʻi Kahoʻolawe Total Kiwikiu 1,500 500 500 0 0 2,500 ʻĀkohekohe 4,500 1,500 1,500 0 0 7,500 ʻIʻiwi X X X 0 0 50,000 Maui ʻalauahio X X X 0 0 99,000 \"X\" means the species is expected in that area, with the total spread across all three."}, {"page": "Forest birds", "title": "Recovery footprint and priority footprint", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html#recovery-footprint-and-priority-footprint", "text": "Forest birds followed the plan's expert-led pathway. Instead of using a computer algorithm, the Forest Bird Team used published literature, recovery plans and professional expertise to map the recovery footprint: the habitat that must be managed to meet population targets on East Maui, West Maui and Molokaʻi. The team also identified a priority footprint for near-term action. In the plan's map, the priority area and the initial implementation area are the same. The plan describes it as the habitat needing management action, specifically mosquito control downslope from current populations. The "}, {"page": "Forest birds", "title": "Priority conservation actions", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html#priority-conservation-actions", "text": "The plan's action prioritization used a modified Priority Threat Management (PTM) process. PTM estimates how much each of 27 strategies (17 single strategies and 10 combinations) would raise the chance that each species group persists over 20 years. For forest birds, only one strategy, the bird-focused combination S27, gives a better than 50 percent chance of persistence after feasibility is accounted for. S27 contains 14 individual strategies. Experts then put those strategies in a recommended order. Recommended order of S27 actions for forest birds Order Strategy What it involves Persistence"}, {"page": "Forest birds", "title": "Monitoring and research needs", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html#monitoring-and-research-needs", "text": "Intensive monitoring. The strategy pairs mosquito suppression with intensive monitoring to judge whether it works and to guide adaptive management. Monitoring priority. Among forest birds, the plan considers kiwikiu and ʻākohekohe the most vulnerable, which calls for more monitoring effort. New tools. Forest birds depend on future methods and technologies to reach 50 percent persistence, unlike Seabirds, Waterbirds and the Hawaiian hoary bat. The top-ranked strategy, S15, is itself the development of new tools against avian malaria. Community engagement. The strategy includes community engagem"}, {"page": "Forest birds", "title": "Island by island", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html#island-by-island", "text": "East Maui. The only place in Maui Nui where these four birds live today. The recovery targets are 1,500 kiwikiu and 4,500 ʻākohekohe. The three emergency mosquito-control areas and the priority footprint are here. West Maui. No at-risk forest birds now. The targets are 500 kiwikiu and 1,500 ʻākohekohe, plus a share of the ʻiʻiwi and Maui ʻalauahio totals. Molokaʻi. Kiwikiu and ʻākohekohe once lived here. The targets are 500 kiwikiu and 1,500 ʻākohekohe, plus a share of the ʻiʻiwi and Maui ʻalauahio totals. Lānaʻi and Kahoʻolawe. No forest bird recovery targets. Beyond Maui Nui. A second ʻākohe"}, {"page": "Seabirds", "title": "The species", "url": "groups/seabirds.html#the-species", "text": "At-risk seabirds in the MNLCP Hawaiian name Common name Scientific name Status Where it nests ʻUaʻu Hawaiian petrel Pterodroma sandwichensis Endangered (State and federal); endemic to Hawaiʻi High-elevation dry habitat with little vegetation, often in lava crevices, and lower-elevation wet, dense native forest ʻAʻo Newell's shearwater Puffinus newelli Threatened (State and federal); endemic to Hawaiʻi Colonies on steep mountain slopes in wet to mesic forest ʻAkēʻakē Band-rumped storm petrel Oceanodroma castro Hawaiʻi's distinct population segment is endangered (State and federal); indigenous N"}, {"page": "Seabirds", "title": "Status and trends", "url": "groups/seabirds.html#status-and-trends", "text": "Introduced mammalian predators, especially rats, mongoose and cats, together with habitat loss, have greatly reduced the abundance of Maui Nui seabirds. Seabirds have a fairly low chance of persisting with no conservation action, but current conservation efforts already give them, along with Waterbirds, better than average persistence. Current estimated breeding pairs Species East Maui West Maui Molokaʻi Lānaʻi Kahoʻolawe Total ʻUaʻu (Hawaiian petrel) 4,000 100-200 1-50 2,000 0 6,101-6,750 ʻAʻo (Newell's shearwater) 50-100 50-100 1-50 1-50 0 102-300 ʻAkēʻakē (band-rumped storm petrel) 50-100 5"}, {"page": "Seabirds", "title": "Threats", "url": "groups/seabirds.html#threats", "text": "Introduced predators. Rats, mongoose and cats prey on nesting seabirds. Habitat loss. Loss of nesting habitat has contributed to the decline. Infrastructure. Artificial lights at night, power lines, wind turbines and roads harm seabird survival."}, {"page": "Seabirds", "title": "Emergency actions", "url": "groups/seabirds.html#emergency-actions", "text": "No seabirds are currently considered to face an imminent threat of extinction, so no emergency actions are listed for this group."}, {"page": "Seabirds", "title": "Goals", "url": "groups/seabirds.html#goals", "text": "The plan aims to roughly double ʻuaʻu breeding pairs and to raise ʻakēʻakē and ʻaʻo numbers by an order of magnitude. The targets are spread across East Maui, West Maui, Molokaʻi, Lānaʻi and Kahoʻolawe. Recovery targets (breeding pairs) Species East Maui West Maui Molokaʻi Lānaʻi Kahoʻolawe Total ʻUaʻu (Hawaiian petrel) 8,000 4,000 1,000 4,000 1,000 18,000 ʻAʻo (Newell's shearwater) 1,000 800 600 600 600 3,600 ʻAkēʻakē (band-rumped storm petrel) 500 400 300 300 300 1,800"}, {"page": "Seabirds", "title": "Recovery footprint and priority footprint", "url": "groups/seabirds.html#recovery-footprint-and-priority-footprint", "text": "Seabirds followed the plan's expert-led pathway. Like the Forest Bird Team, the Seabird Team used published literature, recovery plans and professional expertise to map the recovery footprint needed to sustain seabird populations on all four Maui Nui islands. Offshore islets are part of the recovery footprint, even where they are too small to show on the map. A subset of planning units (areas of about 2 km² used to organize the plan's maps) was chosen as the priority area for conservation action. In the plan's map, the priority area and the initial implementation area are the same. Seabird rec"}, {"page": "Seabirds", "title": "Priority conservation actions", "url": "groups/seabirds.html#priority-conservation-actions", "text": "The plan's action prioritization used a modified Priority Threat Management (PTM) process. PTM estimates how much each of 27 strategies would raise the chance that each species group persists over 20 years. Like Forest birds, Seabirds gain the most from the bird-focused combination strategy S27. Experts ranked predator control actions, such as fencing and predator removal, as the actions to do first. Programs that rescue injured birds, and habitat management, are also high priorities. Recommended order of S27 actions for seabirds Order Strategy What it involves Persistence on its own (%) 1 S10"}, {"page": "Seabirds", "title": "Monitoring and research needs", "url": "groups/seabirds.html#monitoring-and-research-needs", "text": "Recruitment surveys. The fencing strategies include yearly surveys to measure how many translocated or socially attracted birds join fenced colonies. Indirect indicators. The plan gives burrow counts as an example of an indirect indicator for seabird monitoring. Ungulate fencing and habitat work. Existing and future ungulate fencing (S2, S3) and terrestrial habitat management (S5) are also high-priority supporting actions."}, {"page": "Seabirds", "title": "Island by island", "url": "groups/seabirds.html#island-by-island", "text": "East Maui. The largest current ʻuaʻu colony (about 4,000 pairs). Targets: 8,000 ʻuaʻu, 1,000 ʻaʻo and 500 ʻakēʻakē pairs. West Maui. Currently 100-200 ʻuaʻu pairs. Targets: 4,000 ʻuaʻu, 800 ʻaʻo and 400 ʻakēʻakē pairs. Molokaʻi. Currently 1-50 pairs of each species. Targets: 1,000 ʻuaʻu, 600 ʻaʻo and 300 ʻakēʻakē pairs. Lānaʻi. About 2,000 ʻuaʻu pairs today. Targets: 4,000 ʻuaʻu, 600 ʻaʻo and 300 ʻakēʻakē pairs. Kahoʻolawe. No breeding pairs today. Targets: 1,000 ʻuaʻu, 600 ʻaʻo and 300 ʻakēʻakē pairs. S11 includes removing rodents from the whole island, and S7 includes removing cats from it. "}, {"page": "Waterbirds", "title": "The species", "url": "groups/waterbirds.html#the-species", "text": "At-risk waterbirds in the MNLCP Hawaiian name Common name Scientific name Status Habitat In Maui Nui today? ʻAlae keʻokeʻo Hawaiian coot Fulica alai Endangered (State and federal) Wetland ponds and marshes Yes ʻAeʻo Hawaiian stilt Himantopus mexicanus knudseni Endangered (State and federal) Shallow lowland wetlands, mudflats and coastal areas Yes Nēnē Hawaiian goose Branta sandvicensis Federally threatened; State endangered Lowland and upland grassland and shrubland Yes ʻAlae ʻula Hawaiian moorhen Gallinula galeata sandvicensis Endangered (State and federal) Freshwater wetlands with aquatic pl"}, {"page": "Waterbirds", "title": "Status and trends", "url": "groups/waterbirds.html#status-and-trends", "text": "In general, waterbirds have responded well to management. Along with Seabirds, they already have better than average persistence with current conservation efforts. Two species are missing. The ʻalae ʻula needs low-salinity freshwater wetlands, which are rare in Maui Nui. All ducks that resemble kōloa maoli appear to be hybrids with feral mallards. Current estimated populations (individuals) Species Maui Molokaʻi Lānaʻi Kahoʻolawe Total ʻAlae keʻokeʻo (Hawaiian coot) 320 30 14 0 364 ʻAeʻo (Hawaiian stilt) 499 142 43 0 684 Nēnē (Hawaiian goose) 580 30 0 0 610 ʻAlae ʻula (Hawaiian moorhen) 0 0 0 "}, {"page": "Waterbirds", "title": "Threats", "url": "groups/waterbirds.html#threats", "text": "Wetland loss. Human changes to wetlands have destroyed habitat. Introduced mammalian predators. Mongoose, cats, rats and domestic dogs. Botulism. Botulism poisoning is a local threat in some wetlands. Hybridization. Interbreeding between kōloa maoli and feral mallards is a highly significant threat. Human-caused deaths. For nēnē, the plan includes reducing human-caused mortality, such as car strikes."}, {"page": "Waterbirds", "title": "Emergency actions", "url": "groups/waterbirds.html#emergency-actions", "text": "None of the waterbirds is facing imminent extinction, so no emergency actions are listed for this group."}, {"page": "Waterbirds", "title": "Strategy by species", "url": "groups/waterbirds.html#strategy-by-species", "text": "ʻAlae keʻokeʻo and ʻaeʻo. Keep habitat suitable by managing water levels and flow, sediment and invasive weeds, and control predators, using predator-proof fencing where it is cost-effective. Nēnē. Control predators, restore upland areas and increase the population on Molokaʻi. ʻAlae ʻula. Protect and restore suitable habitat, then reintroduce the species to Molokaʻi. Kōloa maoli. First develop an effective way to remove the threat of hybridization. Once that works, it will be feasible to reintroduce kōloa maoli to Maui Nui."}, {"page": "Waterbirds", "title": "Goals", "url": "groups/waterbirds.html#goals", "text": "The recovery footprint includes very important wetlands (and uplands for nēnē) as primary habitat for the populations needed to meet recovery requirements, plus supporting areas that provide additional habitat. Recovery population targets (individuals) Species Maui Molokaʻi Lānaʻi Kahoʻolawe Total ʻAlae keʻokeʻo (Hawaiian coot) X X X 0 750 ʻAeʻo (Hawaiian stilt) X X X 0 750 Nēnē (Hawaiian goose) 2 populations (800) 200 0 0 1,000 ʻAlae ʻula (Hawaiian moorhen) 250 250 Kōloa maoli (Hawaiian duck) X 250 X 0 250 \"X\" means the target can be met on that island as part of the Maui Nui total. The nēnē "}, {"page": "Waterbirds", "title": "Recovery footprint and priority footprint", "url": "groups/waterbirds.html#recovery-footprint-and-priority-footprint", "text": "Waterbirds followed the plan's expert-led pathway. The Waterbird Team and species experts identified the key wetlands needed to provide habitat for all five waterbirds. A subset of wetlands (and uplands for nēnē) in the footprint makes up the priority footprint, where initial conservation actions should focus. In the plan's maps, priority areas and implementation areas are the same. Waterbird recovery footprint (green) with the priority footprint (magenta) nested inside it. The map combines all five waterbirds, including nēnē. Release-candidate footprints of September 25, 2026, as loaded in th"}, {"page": "Waterbirds", "title": "Priority conservation actions", "url": "groups/waterbirds.html#priority-conservation-actions", "text": "The plan's action prioritization used a modified Priority Threat Management (PTM) process. PTM estimates how much each of 27 strategies would raise the chance that each species group persists over 20 years. For ʻalae keʻokeʻo and ʻaeʻo, the most important actions are predator-proof fencing, wetland habitat protection and habitat management. For nēnē, they are restoration, predator control and reducing human-caused deaths. Recommended order of S27 actions for waterbirds Order Strategy What it involves Persistence on its own (%) 1 S10 New predator-proof fencing (against rodents, mongoose and cat"}, {"page": "Waterbirds", "title": "Monitoring and research needs", "url": "groups/waterbirds.html#monitoring-and-research-needs", "text": "Disease surveillance. Continue surveillance, planning and response for avian influenza and other diseases. Standard monitoring. Standardize monitoring of waterbird populations at managed sites. Hybridization research. Develop methods to prevent kōloa maoli from hybridizing with feral mallards. Reintroduction cannot happen until these methods reduce hybridization to an acceptable level. Reintroduction sites. Create and maintain good habitat as potential reintroduction sites for ʻalae ʻula."}, {"page": "Waterbirds", "title": "Island by island", "url": "groups/waterbirds.html#island-by-island", "text": "Maui. Home to most of today's coots (320), stilts (499) and nēnē (580). The nēnē target here is two populations totaling 800 birds (600 and 200). Molokaʻi. Has 30 coots, 142 stilts and 30 nēnē today. The plan aims to increase nēnē here to 200 and to reintroduce ʻalae ʻula (250 birds). The kōloa maoli target also lists 250 birds for Molokaʻi. Lānaʻi. Has 14 coots and 43 stilts today, and can contribute to the Maui Nui-wide coot, stilt and kōloa maoli targets. Kahoʻolawe. No waterbirds today and no waterbird targets."}, {"page": "Plants", "title": "The species", "url": "groups/plants.html#the-species", "text": "Appendix 4 of the plan lists all 197 at-risk plants. It sorts them into priority tiers that the plan also uses to set monitoring effort: At-risk plant tiers Tier Meaning Species PEPP Plant Extinction Prevention Program species: fewer than 50 individuals in the wild, facing imminent extinction 102 POP Species with few populations 30 ROI Species that are rare on an island 19 Insufficient data Not enough information to identify recovery areas 2 Most at-risk plants are listed as endangered. Two species lacked enough information to identify recovery areas, so 195 of the 197 were included in the rec"}, {"page": "Plants", "title": "Some of the at-risk plants", "url": "groups/plants.html#some-of-the-at-risk-plants", "text": "The at-risk list includes many native lobelioids, such as haha ( Cyanea ) and ʻoha wai ( Clermontia ), as well as koʻokoʻolau ( Bidens ), haʻiwale ( Cyrtandra ) and nohoanu ( Geranium ). Examples from the plan's species lists Hawaiian name Scientific name Status Tier Known occupied planning units Pua ʻala Brighamia rockii Endangered PEPP 7 Kokiʻo Kokia cookei Endangered PEPP 0 ʻOha wai Clermontia oblongifolia subsp. mauiensis Endangered PEPP 28 Haha Cyanea mceldowneyi Endangered POP 14 ʻAhinahina Argyroxiphium sandwicense subsp. macrocephalum Threatened none 27"}, {"page": "Plants", "title": "Status and trends", "url": "groups/plants.html#status-and-trends", "text": "Plants are the most data-rich group in the plan. Sources include population data from the Plant Extinction Prevention Program (PEPP), federal recovery plans, the Hawaiʻi State Wildlife Action Plan, standardized recovery goals from the Hawaiʻi and Pacific Plant Recovery Coordinating Committee (HPPRCC), a pilot habitat prioritization study on East Maui, and a study of how climate change may shift plant distributions (Fortini et al. 2013). PEPP tracks the location and status of 685 known sites across Maui Nui. All 102 imminent-extinction species are already being addressed by PEPP. Plants are the"}, {"page": "Plants", "title": "Threats", "url": "groups/plants.html#threats", "text": "Plants face many threats. The most important are habitat loss and invasive species. The plan names these specific threats and the number of at-risk plants each one affects: Landscape-level threats needing new control techniques Threat At-risk plant species affected Slugs 101 Landslides and erosion 96 Drought 79 Flooding 60 Loss of pollinators 44 Black twig borer 21 Ants 7 Coconut rhinoceros beetle 1 Naio thrips 1 Rapid ʻŌhiʻa Death and other ʻōhiʻa diseases not given Other threats the strategy addresses are ungulates (introduced hoofed animals), invasive weeds, rats, invertebrate pests, wildfi"}, {"page": "Plants", "title": "Emergency actions to prevent extinction", "url": "groups/plants.html#emergency-actions-to-prevent-extinction", "text": "Emergency actions are needed for the 102 plants facing imminent extinction: survey, monitor, collect seeds and propagules, control local threats, and propagate and outplant. PEPP focuses on native plants with fewer than 50 individuals left in the wild. It follows standard procedures to address local threats from rats, slugs and invasive weeds. It collects seeds, cuttings and other tissue for genetic storage or seed banking and, when possible, outplants nursery-grown plants back into the wild. The main need is to expand PEPP and DOFAW capacity on Maui, Molokaʻi and Lānaʻi to: - connect stand-al"}, {"page": "Plants", "title": "Goals", "url": "groups/plants.html#goals", "text": "The plan set initial recovery targets for plants by reviewing published recovery plans and consulting experts and the interagency HPPRCC. The goals take into account multi-island distributions, plant life histories and how widely each species is distributed. Appendix 4 gives each species' target number of recovery populations. The full recovery footprint provides for up to 10 populations of each at-risk plant. To meet the goals, the plan calls for managing the plant recovery footprint to: - remove ungulates from recovery habitat and supporting ecosystem areas; - control key invasive weeds, rat"}, {"page": "Plants", "title": "Recovery footprint and priority footprint", "url": "groups/plants.html#recovery-footprint-and-priority-footprint", "text": "Unlike the bird groups, Plants followed the plan's algorithmic pathway. Footprints were selected with OptimTFE, the plan's spatial prioritization algorithm, and then reviewed and refined by experts. The plan gives four reasons this suits plants: the group is very large (197 species), there is a great deal of information about it, there are a very large number of possible places to act, and a dedicated group of experts was available to guide and review the results. The process produced four nested footprints: Plant conservation footprints Footprint What it covers Planning units Area (km²) Recov"}, {"page": "Plants", "title": "Priority conservation actions", "url": "groups/plants.html#priority-conservation-actions", "text": "The plan's action prioritization used a modified Priority Threat Management (PTM) process. PTM estimates how much each of 27 strategies would raise the chance that each species group persists over 20 years. For plants, the plant- and invertebrate-focused combination strategy S25 is the only strategy that raises persistence above 40 percent. Plant groups come close to 50 percent only through the combined effect of many actions, so it matters to find combinations that work well together. S25 actions ranked by benefit to plants, with the recommended order Strategy What it involves Persistence on "}, {"page": "Plants", "title": "Monitoring and research needs", "url": "groups/plants.html#monitoring-and-research-needs", "text": "Data systems. Link current information on population status and trends to a mapped (spatially explicit) data system. Who will maintain and own the database still needs to be decided. Species occurrence and monitoring information should be brought into mapped databases. Pollinators. Develop a strategy to provide pollinators for plant populations that need bird pollinators. Surveys and monitoring. Survey 95 percent of potential habitat and monitor at least one population of each species every year. Climate. Assess ongoing climate change to decide whether replacement recovery habitat is needed. N"}, {"page": "Plants", "title": "Island by island", "url": "groups/plants.html#island-by-island", "text": "The plan does not break plant numbers down by island in Chapter 6. Recovery targets were set by mauna (mountain mass), and the footprint keeps each species represented across the islands and mountain masses where it occurs. Expanded PEPP and DOFAW capacity is needed on Maui, Molokaʻi and Lānaʻi, and S13 includes a rare plant propagation facility on each of those three islands."}, {"page": "Terrestrial snails", "title": "Why snails are in crisis", "url": "groups/terrestrial-snails.html#why-snails-are-in-crisis", "text": "The situation for native land snails, kāhuli, is dire. Hawaiʻi once supported at least 750 native land snail species, and only 10 to 35 percent may still survive. Many of those that remain are at risk of extinction. The Snail Taxonomic Team determined that all 49 at-risk snail species on Maui Nui face imminent extinction. Because of this, the plan focuses first on preventing these losses through a network of exclosures. Broader habitat recovery is not possible yet. Without extinction prevention there will be no snails to recover. Keeping three populations of every species safe is the plan's in"}, {"page": "Terrestrial snails", "title": "The species", "url": "groups/terrestrial-snails.html#the-species", "text": "The 49 at-risk snails belong to nine genera. More than half (29) are in the genus Partulina . Others include Newcombia , Perdicella , Laminella , Pleuropoma , Amastra , Leptachatina , Auriculella and Lyropupa . Many survive in only one or two known wild populations, and two have none known in the wild. Three species are listed as endangered: Newcomb's tree snail ( Newcombia cumingi ) and two Lānaʻi tree snails ( Partulina semicarinata and Partulina variabilis ). The other 46 are not listed, but experts found them just as close to extinction. This is a clear example of why the plan looks beyond"}, {"page": "Terrestrial snails", "title": "Status and trends", "url": "groups/terrestrial-snails.html#status-and-trends", "text": "Snails are declining fast statewide, and safe habitat outside exclosures keeps shrinking. Occurrence data are sparse for many species, so the plan calls for field surveys to find remaining wild populations."}, {"page": "Terrestrial snails", "title": "Threats", "url": "groups/terrestrial-snails.html#threats", "text": "Rosy wolfsnail ( Euglandina rosea ). This introduced predatory snail is the most important threat. There are no proven methods yet to control it across a landscape. Other introduced predators. These include the predatory snail Oxychilus alliarius , Jackson's chameleon and rats. Habitat threats at future recovery sites. When habitat restoration begins, recovery sites will also need fire management, biosecurity and infrastructure, and removal of key weeds such as strawberry guava. Like forest birds and avian malaria, snails face one overwhelming threat. Until rosy wolfsnail can be controlled acr"}, {"page": "Terrestrial snails", "title": "Recovery strategy", "url": "groups/terrestrial-snails.html#recovery-strategy", "text": "The strategy's long-term aim is to restore every surviving species to healthy, self-sustaining populations in the wild. For now, the focus is on preventing extinction."}, {"page": "Terrestrial snails", "title": "Goals", "url": "groups/terrestrial-snails.html#goals", "text": "Interim recovery goal: keep three populations of each of the 49 species in predator-proof exclosures (kīpuka kāhuli), and in laboratory rearing facilities if needed. Next step: expand populations outside kīpuka kāhuli. Deferred: landscape-level habitat management to recover snails and remove them from the at-risk list. This waits until there are workable ways to control rosy wolfsnail."}, {"page": "Terrestrial snails", "title": "Emergency actions to prevent extinction", "url": "groups/terrestrial-snails.html#emergency-actions-to-prevent-extinction", "text": "The plan's snail objectives are: - Rescue populations that could be eliminated within five years by rosy wolfsnail, rats or chameleons. Move them into laboratory captive rearing and/or kīpuka kāhuli. - Build a network of 32 kīpuka kāhuli that protects at least three populations of each species. Build first where the most at-risk species are, and place sites across Maui Nui's climates so the network can hold all 49 species cost-effectively. - Establish an ex situ captive rearing program and laboratory on Maui that can house all 49 species. - Research ways to control invasive predators, especial"}, {"page": "Terrestrial snails", "title": "The kīpuka kāhuli network (preventing extinction footprint)", "url": "groups/terrestrial-snails.html#the-kīpuka-kāhuli-network-preventing-extinction-footprint", "text": "A kīpuka kāhuli is a small, managed refuge enclosed by a predator-proof fence that keeps out invasive predators. Unlike other groups, the snail footprint is a network of exclosure sites, not a recovery footprint. Its job is to prevent extinction while keeping options open for future recovery planning. The network was developed in three steps: 1. Finding potential sites. Snails followed the plan's algorithmic pathway, using the OptimTFE prioritization algorithm. Candidate sites were judged on elevation, slope, ruggedness, native habitat status, fire risk, climate resilience and habitat structur"}, {"page": "Terrestrial snails", "title": "Priority conservation actions", "url": "groups/terrestrial-snails.html#priority-conservation-actions", "text": "The plan's action prioritization (a modified Priority Threat Management, or PTM, analysis, led by UH Mānoa) estimated how much each strategy would raise the chance that snails persist for the plan's 20-year timeframe. Snail survival depends heavily on countering one threat, rosy wolfsnail predation. If effective control methods are found and used, the chance that snails persist rises above 50 percent. Suggested sequencing of Strategy 25 actions to benefit snails (plan Table 18). The plan's caption says that if all actions are implemented, snails could have up to a 63 percent chance of persisti"}, {"page": "Terrestrial snails", "title": "Surveys and data gaps", "url": "groups/terrestrial-snails.html#surveys-and-data-gaps", "text": "Survey for more populations of at-risk species. Survey for areas without rosy wolfsnail, to support search-and-rescue work that secures strong genetic representation of each species. Identify recovery habitat for each of the 49 species, plus the landing zones and staging campsites needed to reach it. Update the exclosure network as surveys find more populations."}, {"page": "Terrestrial snails", "title": "Monitoring and research needs", "url": "groups/terrestrial-snails.html#monitoring-and-research-needs", "text": "Landscape-level control of key threats: rosy wolfsnail, Jackson's chameleon and rats. Ex situ methods: ways to store genetic material and to propagate all at-risk snail species. Population records: databases on wild and captive populations. Infrastructure: the Maui laboratory and propagation capacity under S13."}, {"page": "Terrestrial snails", "title": "How this fits the whole plan", "url": "groups/terrestrial-snails.html#how-this-fits-the-whole-plan", "text": "The 49 snails have sites identified for kīpuka kāhuli but no mapped recovery habitat yet, so the plan's combined recovery footprint figures count them separately. Snail exclosure sites can be viewed with all other group footprints in the Conservation Planner. Image to come: Photo of a native Maui Nui tree snail (e.g., Partulina) on a leaf, or a kīpuka kāhuli fence in native forest Suggested source: DOFAW / Snail Extinction Prevention Program photo library (not in plan)"}, {"page": "Terrestrial arthropods", "title": "Why arthropods matter, and why they are hard to plan for", "url": "groups/terrestrial-arthropods.html#why-arthropods-matter-and-why-they-are-hard-to-plan-for", "text": "Terrestrial arthropods are the plan's second-largest group after plants. The group has thousands of species and very high levels of endemism, and it is generally poorly known. Many more species than the 172 may also be at risk of extinction. Many at-risk arthropods lack enough distribution data (usually fewer than five occurrence records) or information on habitat needs to show where to focus management. For these species, the plan sets up surveys and research to collect the data, and then adds them to the plan's footprints. Of 172 at-risk arthropods, only 32 have enough data to map where reco"}, {"page": "Terrestrial arthropods", "title": "The species", "url": "groups/terrestrial-arthropods.html#the-species", "text": "The at-risk list spans many arthropod groups. The largest are picture-winged flies ( Drosophila , 36 species) and yellow-faced bees ( Hylaeus , 22). They are followed by Plagithmysus (18), Philodoria (11) and Nesosydne (8), along with more than 40 other genera. The group also includes hawk moths and leafhoppers. The eight endangered species are two picture-winged flies ( Drosophila differens , D. neoclavisetae ), five yellow-faced bees ( Hylaeus anthracinus , H. assimulans , H. facilis , H. hilaris , H. longiceps ) and Blackburn's sphinx moth ( Manduca blackburni ). The 32 species with mappabl"}, {"page": "Terrestrial arthropods", "title": "Status and trends", "url": "groups/terrestrial-arthropods.html#status-and-trends", "text": "The 172 species vary widely in how much is known about them. Many lack modern occurrence records. Some can only be placed by where their host plants grow or where conditions are broadly suitable, which gives wide and uncertain ranges. Because the data are so limited, the plan does not give trends for the group as a whole."}, {"page": "Terrestrial arthropods", "title": "Threats", "url": "groups/terrestrial-arthropods.html#threats", "text": "Habitat loss and ecosystem degradation. Non-native wasps and ants, through predation and competition. Host-plant decline. Insects that depend on one host plant species are at risk if that plant declines or goes extinct. Ungulates, weeds and rats in recovery habitat; in some areas, rodents (mice) damage host plants."}, {"page": "Terrestrial arthropods", "title": "Goals", "url": "groups/terrestrial-arthropods.html#goals", "text": "The plan's initial target is five healthy, self-sustaining populations for each arthropod species. Thirty-two species had enough occurrence and habitat information to map recovery habitat. The other 140 did not. The recovery footprint covers the interim recovery habitat needs of 31 species and the full recovery needs of one, Blackburn's sphinx moth."}, {"page": "Terrestrial arthropods", "title": "Emergency actions for nine yellow-faced bees", "url": "groups/terrestrial-arthropods.html#emergency-actions-for-nine-yellow-faced-bees", "text": "Nine yellow-faced bee species ( Hylaeus ) face imminent extinction within five years. Four of them are listed under federal and state law. A fifth listed species, Hylaeus assimulans , is not at imminent risk. The main actions to prevent these extinctions are habitat protection (fencing) and restoring native vegetation. Existing fences should be maintained. Every one of the nine should have at least one extant or recently occupied site targeted for emergency action, such as ant control, fire protection or outplanting host plants. Yellow-faced bees facing imminent extinction in the next five yea"}, {"page": "Terrestrial arthropods", "title": "Recovery footprint and priority footprint", "url": "groups/terrestrial-arthropods.html#recovery-footprint-and-priority-footprint", "text": "The recovery footprint shows where to restore and manage habitat for the 32 species with enough data. Management there means removing ungulates and controlling key invasive weeds, rats and invertebrate pests. The plan also identifies a priority footprint: areas with populations of the nine species facing imminent extinction. Terrestrial arthropods followed the plan's algorithmic pathway. The recovery footprint was built with the plan's comprehensive planning process, TFE-SCP (Transparent, Flexible, Expert-engaged Systematic Conservation Planning), and its OptimTFE prioritization algorithm, the"}, {"page": "Terrestrial arthropods", "title": "Priority conservation actions", "url": "groups/terrestrial-arthropods.html#priority-conservation-actions", "text": "The plan's action prioritization (a modified Priority Threat Management, or PTM, analysis, led by UH Mānoa) assessed three arthropod subgroups: yellow-faced bees ( Hylaeus ), picture-winged flies ( Drosophila ) and Blackburn's sphinx moth ( Manduca ). (The PTM also assessed Megalagrion damselflies, which the plan groups with Aquatic invertebrates.) With the extensive plant- and invertebrate-focused management in Strategy S25, arthropods as a group may reach a 65 percent chance of persistence. When adjusted for feasibility, this drops to 58 percent. All three subgroups reach between the mid-50s"}, {"page": "Terrestrial arthropods", "title": "Surveys and data gaps", "url": "groups/terrestrial-arthropods.html#surveys-and-data-gaps", "text": "For the 140 species without enough data, the plan's spatial prioritization team (led by USGS) and the Terrestrial Arthropod Team used TFE-SCP and OptimTFE to map priority areas for at-risk arthropod surveys. The teams used host-plant associations, habitat quality, accessibility and other criteria to suggest where multi-species surveys should be considered. The survey map is not a recovery footprint. It shows where field surveys are most likely to find new populations, and so produce the data needed for long-term management plans. The plan uses two kinds of surveys: - Area surveys (Focal Area S"}, {"page": "Terrestrial arthropods", "title": "Monitoring and research needs", "url": "groups/terrestrial-arthropods.html#monitoring-and-research-needs", "text": "Propagation facilities for invertebrates. Review of listed and well-documented species to find other species facing imminent extinction. New control tools for ants and wasps, under S13. More invertebrate expertise. Experts made fewer predictions for plants and invertebrates than for vertebrates. This reflects a need for more plant and invertebrate expertise in the state, especially within DLNR, and gaps in knowledge about the distribution and abundance of native invertebrates."}, {"page": "Aquatic invertebrates", "title": "The species", "url": "groups/aquatic-invertebrates.html#the-species", "text": "The group combines two very different kinds of habitat. Seven species live in anchialine pools. The other four are more often found in streams, seeps or wetlands. Anchialine pools are landlocked water bodies with an underground connection to the ocean through porous volcanic rock. They are found in only a few parts of the world and support unique animals. Hawaiian damselflies ( Megalagrion ). Three at-risk species, all endangered: the flying earwig Hawaiian damselfly ( M. nesiotes ), the Pacific Hawaiian damselfly ( M. pacificum ) and the orangeblack Hawaiian damselfly ( M. xanthomelas ). Anch"}, {"page": "Aquatic invertebrates", "title": "Status and trends", "url": "groups/aquatic-invertebrates.html#status-and-trends", "text": "Most of these species are known from very few places. Five of the anchialine species are known from a single planning unit each. The plan does not give population sizes or trends for the group."}, {"page": "Aquatic invertebrates", "title": "Threats", "url": "groups/aquatic-invertebrates.html#threats", "text": "Habitat loss, including reduced stream flow where water is diverted. Aquatic invasive species: introduced fish and frogs (vertebrates) and shrimp (invertebrates) that prey on native species. Feral pigs, ants and other non-native insects, for the flying earwig Hawaiian damselfly. Natural disasters and climate change, which threaten species confined to very small areas."}, {"page": "Aquatic invertebrates", "title": "Emergency action: the flying earwig Hawaiian damselfly", "url": "groups/aquatic-invertebrates.html#emergency-action-the-flying-earwig-hawaiian-damselfly", "text": "Megalagrion nesiotes is the only aquatic invertebrate facing imminent extinction. It is a dark-colored, weak flier of deep forest that usually rests on vegetation and on steep, moist, fern-covered banks. Its young (naiads) are thought to live on land or partly on land, in damp leaf litter. It is listed as endangered and historically was found only on Maui and Hawaiʻi Island, with a single population on each. Actions needed: protect the known site from ungulate damage, prevent predation by invasive species, and monitor at least once a year. The plan also calls for preventing the introduction of"}, {"page": "Aquatic invertebrates", "title": "Goals", "url": "groups/aquatic-invertebrates.html#goals", "text": "Early planning aimed for 10 populations of each species. These targets were adjusted for species found on several islands and for how much habitat is available. As a result, five species should be able to reach full recovery goals, five have interim recovery goals of one population, and one has no identified recovery area."}, {"page": "Aquatic invertebrates", "title": "Recovery footprint", "url": "groups/aquatic-invertebrates.html#recovery-footprint", "text": "Aquatic invertebrates followed the plan's expert-led pathway. The Aquatic Invertebrate Team and other species experts drew the recovery footprint directly, without an algorithm. They used occurrence data, recovery plans, species reports, Hawaiʻi's State Wildlife Action Plan and scientific literature to map a recovery footprint for 10 of the 11 species. Expert-led delineation was used because the group has few focal species and strong existing expert knowledge. The footprint covers the interim recovery habitat needs of 5 species and the full recovery needs of 5 species. It does not yet include "}, {"page": "Aquatic invertebrates", "title": "Restoring habitat", "url": "groups/aquatic-invertebrates.html#restoring-habitat", "text": "Restoring and managing habitat for all 11 species means managing the recovery footprint to: 1. Set and keep minimum instream flows, without helping invasive aquatic species spread. 2. Control invasive aquatic species. 3. Manage wetland habitats for at-risk species. 4. Control invasive invertebrate species. 5. Build and maintain ungulate fencing. The plan also calls for future tools for rare plants and invertebrates."}, {"page": "Aquatic invertebrates", "title": "Priority conservation actions", "url": "groups/aquatic-invertebrates.html#priority-conservation-actions", "text": "The plan's action prioritization (a modified Priority Threat Management, or PTM, analysis, led by UH Mānoa) assessed three aquatic subgroups: Megalagrion damselflies, Procaris (anchialine pool shrimp) and Lymnaeidae (freshwater snails). Combined, aquatic invertebrates may reach an average chance of persistence above 50 percent. Megalagrion and Lymnaeidae did best under Strategy 25, a plant- and invertebrate-focused combination. Procaris did slightly better (by one percentage point) under the bird-focused Strategy 27. The three subgroups respond differently, so the plan suggests that separate a"}, {"page": "Aquatic invertebrates", "title": "Surveys and data gaps", "url": "groups/aquatic-invertebrates.html#surveys-and-data-gaps", "text": "Freshwater pond snails (Lymnaeidae) have no identified recovery area. They are assigned to species-specific surveys. Surveys are part of the emergency response for Megalagrion nesiotes ."}, {"page": "Aquatic invertebrates", "title": "Monitoring and research needs", "url": "groups/aquatic-invertebrates.html#monitoring-and-research-needs", "text": "Monitor the Megalagrion nesiotes population at least once a year. Monitor lowland streams continuously to keep them free of invasive species. Evaluate existing barriers and diversions on key streams, and plan for them before they fail or are removed. Develop new tools to control invasive aquatic species and ants, under S13."}, {"page": "Hawaiian hoary bat", "title": "The species", "url": "groups/hawaiian-hoary-bat.html#the-species", "text": "The Hawaiian hoary bat occurs on Maui, Molokaʻi and Lānaʻi, and it has been observed on Kahoʻolawe. It is not known whether these are three (or four) separate populations or a single connected population (metapopulation). Bats usually roost in trees and forage across many habitats, from native and non-native forests to wetlands and riparian areas. They have been found roosting in ʻōhiʻa, pūhala, coconut palms, kukui, kiawe, avocado, shower trees, pūkiawe, fern clumps, eucalyptus, Cook pine and Norfolk Island pine stands. They feed mainly on night-flying beetles and moths. Unlike the seven othe"}, {"page": "Hawaiian hoary bat", "title": "Status and trends", "url": "groups/hawaiian-hoary-bat.html#status-and-trends", "text": "There is no reliable population estimate for bats in Maui Nui. The size and trend of Maui Nui bat populations are unknown. There is some sign that bats on Hawaiʻi Island may be stable or slightly increasing. Experts were optimistic about bat conservation. But because bat population trends cannot yet be measured, it is not yet possible to confirm that conservation actions are helping bat populations."}, {"page": "Hawaiian hoary bat", "title": "Threats", "url": "groups/hawaiian-hoary-bat.html#threats", "text": "Collisions with wind turbines. Habitat loss. Possibly pesticide exposure, and possibly predation by invasive vertebrates such as cats and rats. Predation on bats has been shown on the mainland but not documented in Hawaiʻi."}, {"page": "Hawaiian hoary bat", "title": "Recovery strategy", "url": "groups/hawaiian-hoary-bat.html#recovery-strategy", "text": "The plan's strategy for the bat involves: - Preventing incidental take (accidental killing or harm) of bats from human activities, especially wind energy turbines. - Protecting forested areas and wetlands in the plan's recovery footprints for Forest birds, Seabirds, Waterbirds, Plants, Aquatic invertebrates and Terrestrial arthropods. - Reducing populations of invasive vertebrate predators, especially feral cats and rats, across Maui Nui. - Developing methods to measure population trends. - Identifying actions that can increase the number of young bats raised (productivity). - Finding out whet"}, {"page": "Hawaiian hoary bat", "title": "Goals", "url": "groups/hawaiian-hoary-bat.html#goals", "text": "Setting numeric recovery goals for the bat is hard. The interim recovery goal is to keep the bat at or above its current population level, and to expand forest habitat, especially native forest, across Maui Nui."}, {"page": "Hawaiian hoary bat", "title": "Emergency actions", "url": "groups/hawaiian-hoary-bat.html#emergency-actions", "text": "None are needed. The bat is not at imminent risk of extinction in the next five years."}, {"page": "Hawaiian hoary bat", "title": "Recovery footprint", "url": "groups/hawaiian-hoary-bat.html#recovery-footprint", "text": "The bat is a plan-only group: it was not part of the spatial prioritization, and it has no footprint of its own. Instead, the plan provides forest and wetland habitat for the bat through the native habitats in the recovery footprints for Forest birds, Seabirds, Waterbirds, Plants, Aquatic invertebrates and Terrestrial arthropods. There are no priority footprints for the bat. The plan does not give a planning-unit count for the combined bat footprint. Recovery footprint for the Hawaiian hoary bat, made up of the recovery footprints for Forest birds, Seabirds, Waterbirds, Plants, Aquatic inverte"}, {"page": "Hawaiian hoary bat", "title": "Priority conservation actions", "url": "groups/hawaiian-hoary-bat.html#priority-conservation-actions", "text": "The plan's action prioritization (a modified Priority Threat Management, or PTM, analysis, led by UH Mānoa) estimated how each action might affect the bat's chance of persisting on Maui Nui. Strategy 27, the most bird-focused combination, appears to give the bat the highest chance of persistence: 63 percent if fully implemented. The bat is one of three groups (with Seabirds and Waterbirds) that can reach the plan's 50 percent persistence goal without relying on research or unproven new methods."}, {"page": "Hawaiian hoary bat", "title": "1. Minimize harm from infrastructure (S16)", "url": "groups/hawaiian-hoary-bat.html#1-minimize-harm-from-infrastructure-s16", "text": "The most beneficial single strategy is S16, minimizing and modifying infrastructure, which targets bat deaths at wind energy farms. The plan calls it the most strategic action for two reasons: - There are no accurate population estimates or trends for a population viability analysis (PVA), which would show how many bat deaths each year a population can withstand. - No documented conservation measures offset wind turbine deaths by reducing other causes of bat mortality or by increasing reproduction. Because bat numbers and trends are unknown, avoiding bat deaths at wind turbines is the most str"}, {"page": "Hawaiian hoary bat", "title": "2. Lay the foundation with habitat management (S6 and S5)", "url": "groups/hawaiian-hoary-bat.html#2-lay-the-foundation-with-habitat-management-s6-and-s5", "text": "Stream and wetland habitat management (S6) and terrestrial habitat management (S5) have not been shown to increase bat populations. They do lay the groundwork for ecosystem restoration that may create more roosting and foraging opportunities."}, {"page": "Hawaiian hoary bat", "title": "Also: predator control (S7 and S11)", "url": "groups/hawaiian-hoary-bat.html#also-predator-control-s7-and-s11", "text": "Invasive cats and rats may be an important threat to bats. Limited predator control would happen under S7 (invasive vertebrate pest control) in the Seabird and Forest bird footprints. S11 (landscape-scale rat suppression) would likely have more impact because it covers large landscapes. Suggested sequencing of Strategy 27 actions to benefit Hawaiian hoary bats (plan Table 24). The plan's caption says that if all actions are implemented, bats are expected to have a 63 percent chance of persisting for 20 years. <!-- src: plan p.80, Table 24, lines 3217-3238 --> Strategy Description Persistence ("}, {"page": "Hawaiian hoary bat", "title": "Monitoring and research needs", "url": "groups/hawaiian-hoary-bat.html#monitoring-and-research-needs", "text": "Research and monitoring are central to the bat strategy, because so little is known about bat numbers on Maui Nui. - Develop methods to measure bat population trends. - Get population estimates good enough for a population viability analysis. - Identify actions that can increase productivity in bat populations. - Find out whether the presence or absence of flight lanes in forests matters for bats. - Learn whether Maui Nui bats form three or four separate populations or one metapopulation. - Document whether predation by invasive cats and rats affects bats in Hawaiʻi."}, {"page": "Taxonomic Groups", "title": "Forest birds", "url": "groups/forest-birds.html", "text": "Four at-risk forest birds survive only on East Maui, two facing imminent extinction; controlling mosquito-borne avian malaria is the top priority, followed by returning birds to West Maui and Molokaʻi."}, {"page": "Taxonomic Groups", "title": "Seabirds", "url": "groups/seabirds.html", "text": "Three listed seabirds nest across all four Maui Nui islands; none faces imminent extinction, and predator-proof fencing, predator control and safer infrastructure can bring them above 50 percent persistence with existing tools."}, {"page": "Taxonomic Groups", "title": "Waterbirds", "url": "groups/waterbirds.html", "text": "All five native waterbirds are listed and two are gone from Maui Nui; the plan builds on past success with wetland protection, predator fencing and control, and later reintroductions."}, {"page": "Taxonomic Groups", "title": "Plants", "url": "groups/plants.html", "text": "The largest group, with 197 at-risk plants and 102 facing imminent extinction; emergency PEPP work, a 317-planning-unit recovery footprint prioritized with OptimTFE and refined by experts, and new tools against pests that cannot be controlled today."}, {"page": "Taxonomic Groups", "title": "Terrestrial snails", "url": "groups/terrestrial-snails.html", "text": "All 49 at-risk snails could be lost within five years, so the plan's first goal is to prevent their extinction by keeping three populations of each species safe in 32 predator-proof kīpuka kāhuli and a Maui laboratory."}, {"page": "Taxonomic Groups", "title": "Terrestrial arthropods", "url": "groups/terrestrial-arthropods.html", "text": "Of 172 at-risk native arthropods, only 32 have enough data to map recovery habitat, so the plan pairs emergency protection for nine yellow-faced bees and habitat restoration with targeted surveys for the other 140."}, {"page": "Taxonomic Groups", "title": "Aquatic invertebrates", "url": "groups/aquatic-invertebrates.html", "text": "Eleven at-risk damselflies, anchialine pool shrimp and freshwater snails depend on streams, wetlands and anchialine pools; the plan protects the last Maui Nui site of one damselfly and restores stream flows while keeping invasive species out."}, {"page": "Taxonomic Groups", "title": "Hawaiian hoary bat", "url": "groups/hawaiian-hoary-bat.html", "text": "Maui Nui's only native land mammal is endangered but not at imminent risk; the plan focuses on cutting wind-turbine deaths, protecting forests and wetlands in other groups' footprints, and learning how to measure bat trends."}, {"page": "Conservation Footprints", "title": "Planning units: the building blocks", "url": "footprints.html#planning-units-the-building-blocks", "text": "To map where species can recover, the plan divided the Maui Nui landscape into a network of about 1,400 medium-sized planning units (PUs). Each planning unit is roughly 2 km². Its boundaries follow watersheds, natural barriers, and elevation bands. A planning unit is sized to support a single population of an at-risk species. Footprints are built up as collections of planning units. Each unit records which species it was selected for, how suitable its habitat is for each species, and whether the selection rests on occurrence records or on model predictions."}, {"page": "Conservation Footprints", "title": "Recovery footprints and priority footprints", "url": "footprints.html#recovery-footprints-and-priority-footprints", "text": "The plan produces two main kinds of conservation footprint."}, {"page": "Conservation Footprints", "title": "Recovery footprints", "url": "footprints.html#recovery-footprints", "text": "A recovery footprint is the set of planning units that contain the existing or restorable habitat needed for an at-risk species, or a whole species group, to reach its recovery goals. It shows where active management can support populations as they grow toward recovery."}, {"page": "Conservation Footprints", "title": "Priority footprints", "url": "footprints.html#priority-footprints", "text": "A priority footprint is a smaller subset nested inside a recovery footprint. It identifies priority areas where both preventing extinction and recovery goals can be advanced. Priority footprints advance recovery but are not expected to meet recovery goals on their own. They are generally the most important places to focus MNLCP recovery actions in the near term."}, {"page": "Conservation Footprints", "title": "Initial implementation areas", "url": "footprints.html#initial-implementation-areas", "text": "Some groups have large recovery and priority footprints. For these, the plan narrows the footprint further into suggested starting points for implementation. Recovery footprints show everything species need to recover. Priority footprints show where to start."}, {"page": "Conservation Footprints", "title": "How footprints were made", "url": "footprints.html#how-footprints-were-made", "text": "The footprints come from the plan's spatial prioritization, led by USGS and carried out through the TFE-SCP comprehensive planning process. DOFAW coordinated taxon expert teams, and experts validated and refined draft footprints in collaborative review sessions. [{\"title\": \"Set an explicit recovery target\", \"text\": \"Planning starts with one species and its populations. For the plant Cyanea mceldowneyi, the recovery target is 10 populations of 400 individuals each: 4,000 plants in all.\"}, {\"title\": \"Map suitable habitat\", \"text\": \"Species distribution models, checked on the ground, show where s"}, {"page": "Conservation Footprints", "title": "Combined results across all groups", "url": "footprints.html#combined-results-across-all-groups", "text": "When the footprints of all eight taxonomic groups are combined, the recovery footprint includes 492 planning units covering 1,050 km². It provides recovery habitat for 250 at-risk species. It also holds candidate exclosure sites for all 49 at-risk Terrestrial snails, which have exclosure locations but not recovery habitat, for 299 species in all. From a landscape perspective, the combined recovery footprint: lies mainly on lands already oriented toward conservation (65.7 percent); spans all four Maui Nui islands, with the upland areas of Haleakalā, West Maui, East Molokaʻi, and Lānaʻihale bein"}, {"page": "Conservation Footprints", "title": "Places with many at-risk species", "url": "footprints.html#places-with-many-at-risk-species", "text": "The Adaptive Conservation Planner can highlight planning units with exceptionally high numbers of at-risk species, or units holding species facing imminent extinction. Managers can use this to work more efficiently. Combined priority footprint shaded by the number of at-risk species each planning unit supports. The darkest units are the richest places to act. Release-candidate footprints of September 25, 2026, as loaded in the Adaptive Conservation Planner; unit counts can differ from the plan text. The same view for the full recovery footprint. Release-candidate footprints of September 25, 20"}, {"page": "Conservation Footprints", "title": "What footprints are, and what they are not", "url": "footprints.html#what-footprints-are-and-what-they-are-not", "text": "Footprints are decision support. The MNLCP is state-led and practitioner-driven. It provides flexible guidance to managers rather than top-down prescriptive mandates. Footprints are landscape-scale. They answer \"where should we focus?\" A planning unit may be hundreds of hectares. Exactly where to outplant, fence, or survey within a planning unit is worked out by field teams using finer-scale tools such as Find My Microhabitat. Footprints are living products. They can be updated as conditions, knowledge, and available resources change, and the Conservation Planner is where they are inspected, r"}, {"page": "Conservation Footprints", "title": "Species not yet on the map", "url": "footprints.html#species-not-yet-on-the-map", "text": "There are 143 at-risk species that lack enough information to reliably identify habitat and planning units for their recovery. Most are Terrestrial arthropods. Of the 143, 82 are targeted for multi-species area surveys and 61 for species-specific surveys. The plan sets out targeted surveys to learn these species' habitat needs, so they can be added to the recovery footprints over time. The intent is to identify habitat needs and conservation strategies for all 442 at-risk species."}, {"page": "Conservation Footprints", "title": "How to read the map", "url": "footprints.html#how-to-read-the-map", "text": "Pick a view. Start with all groups combined, or choose a single species group from the layer menu. Switch between recovery and priority. Recovery footprints show the full area needed for recovery. Priority footprints show where to start. Each priority footprint sits inside its recovery footprint. Click a planning unit to see which groups and how many at-risk species it was selected for. Use the richness view to see how many at-risk species each planning unit supports. Darker colors mean more species. Remember the scale. Each shape is a planning unit of about 2 km², not a specific site for fenc"}, {"page": "Conservation Footprints", "title": "Map legend", "url": "footprints.html#map-legend", "text": "Recovery footprint (green): Planning units with existing or restorable habitat needed for the group's at-risk species to reach recovery goals. Priority footprint (yellow): The subset of the recovery footprint where both preventing extinction and recovery goals can be advanced; the most important places to start. Combined footprint (all groups): All species groups' footprints merged into one layer. Overlapping units are counted once. Species richness: Number of at-risk species with targets in each planning unit. Darker shading means more species. Terrestrial snail sites: Candidate kīpuka kāhuli"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "What is the Maui Nui Landscape Conservation Plan?", "url": "faq.html#what-is-mnlcp", "text": "The Maui Nui Landscape Conservation Plan (MNLCP) is the first islands-wide, landscape-scale roadmap showing where conservation actions can most effectively prevent extinctions, recover at-risk species, and restore native ecosystems across Maui, Molokaʻi, Lānaʻi, and Kahoʻolawe. It is a plan of the Hawaiʻi Division of Forestry and Wildlife (DOFAW). It covers native land and freshwater species and e"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Who developed the plan?", "url": "faq.html#who-developed", "text": "DOFAW Maui led the plan. Partners included USGS, USFWS, the University of Hawaiʻi at Mānoa and Hilo, the Bishop Museum, and other land managers and scientists. More than 100 land managers, scientists, and species experts contributed. DOFAW coordinated the taxon expert teams and led the implementation and core teams. Spatial prioritization was led by USGS, action prioritization by UH Mānoa, and eco"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Why is the plan needed?", "url": "faq.html#why-needed", "text": "Hawaiʻi has less than 0.2 percent of the nation's land area but 29 percent of its imperiled species, and more than 60 percent of its native ecosystems have been destroyed or badly degraded. Many good plans already exist, including recovery plans, the State Wildlife Action Plan, and site plans. But they are not connected at the landscape or management level, so managers have to reconcile dozens of "}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "What are the plan's goals, and how long will it run?", "url": "faq.html#goals-timeframe", "text": "The plan has three linked goals: Prevent the extinction of the most imperiled native plants and animals. Recover all at-risk species to healthy, self-sustaining population levels. Protect and restore native ecosystems so they provide habitat for all Maui Nui biodiversity and sustain clean water, cultural values, and local economies. It is designed to be carried out over 20 years. Because it is not"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How is this different from earlier conservation plans?", "url": "faq.html#how-different", "text": "Most earlier plans deal with one species or one site at a time. The MNLCP brings the recovery needs of all at-risk species together into one map-based plan. That plan identifies the set of places where actions do the most good for all species combined. It links three analyses (where to work, what to do, and ecosystem priorities) into one decision-support system. It is also delivered as a living sy"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "What planning approach does the plan use?", "url": "faq.html#approach", "text": "The plan uses an approach called Integrated Multi-Scale Conservation Planning (IMCP). It links three parts: Spatial prioritization (where to work), done through the Transparent, Flexible, Expert-engaged Systematic Conservation Planning (TFE-SCP) process. Action prioritization (what to do), done through a modified Priority Threat Management (PTM) process. Ecosystem prioritization , which looks at t"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How were species experts and managers involved?", "url": "faq.html#experts", "text": "DOFAW coordinated taxon expert teams, and experts took part in collaborative review sessions throughout the process. The teams helped decide which species to include and set recovery targets. They also identified known populations and suitable habitat, chose the most important actions, and identified species facing imminent extinction. Experts reviewed and refined the draft footprints species by s"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How many species are at risk, and what does \"at risk\" mean?", "url": "faq.html#how-many-at-risk", "text": "The plan covers 442 native species: birds, plants, invertebrates, and a bat. Experts consider each one likely to go extinct within the next 20 years without significant management. The main reasons are very low numbers, steep declines, climate change, newly arrived invasive species, and habitat loss. Browse them in the At-Risk Species explorer."}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Why does the plan include species that are not on endangered species lists?", "url": "faq.html#why-unlisted", "text": "Only 202 of the 442 species are listed under state or federal endangered species laws. Species experts identified the other 240 because of very small or declining populations, restricted ranges, or vulnerability to climate change. These unlisted species are treated the same way as listed ones: every species gets recommended actions, and where the data allow, a recovery footprint."}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "What does \"facing imminent extinction\" mean, and what are PEPP species?", "url": "faq.html#imminent-extinction", "text": "In all, 163 of the 442 species could be lost within five years without immediate emergency action. These include 102 plants, all 49 at-risk Terrestrial snails, 9 Terrestrial arthropods, 2 Forest birds, and 1 Aquatic invertebrate. Emergency actions put speed and effectiveness first. Examples are seed banking, captive propagation, very small predator-proof exclosures, and close protection of the las"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Which groups of species does the plan cover?", "url": "faq.html#groups-covered", "text": "The plan covers eight taxonomic groups: Plants (197 species), Terrestrial arthropods (172), Terrestrial snails (49), Aquatic invertebrates (11), Waterbirds (5), Forest birds (4), Seabirds (3), and the Hawaiian hoary bat, or ʻōpeʻapeʻa (1). Seven groups had their own taxon expert team. The bat, Hawaiʻi's only native land mammal, is the eighth group and did not have a dedicated team. For the bat, th"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Why don't some species have a recovery footprint?", "url": "faq.html#no-footprint", "text": "In all, 143 at-risk species still lack the data needed to map recovery habitat, and most of them are Terrestrial arthropods. Many arthropods have fewer than five occurrence records. For these species the plan sets up surveys instead. Area Surveys are based on where the species' host plants grow. Species-specific Surveys are for species where even that information is missing. As surveys find popula"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "What is the difference between \"interim\" and \"full\" recovery goals?", "url": "faq.html#interim-full", "text": "Full recovery goals were set for species with good data on habitat, threats, and effective actions. Reaching them is expected to mean the species is no longer at risk. Many of these goals come from federal recovery plans or from the Hawaiʻi and Pacific Plant Recovery Coordinating Committee. Interim recovery goals were set where data are adequate but known locations are few, habitat restoration wil"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "What happens when a species not on the list becomes at risk, for example when it is newly listed or rediscovered?", "url": "faq.html#add-species", "text": "The plan is built to take in new species and new information. Species that lack data are meant to be added to the plan as information becomes available. The Adaptive Conservation Planner lets authorized users propose new target locations for review and approval. To add a new species, users enter its recovery targets and assign planning units, and colleagues then review the change. A newly found sp"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "What is a conservation footprint?", "url": "faq.html#what-footprint", "text": "A conservation footprint is a map of the planning units chosen for species recovery. The plan uses footprints that nest inside each other: Recovery footprints contain the existing or restorable habitat needed to reach recovery goals. Priority footprints are subsets of the recovery footprint. They mark the most important areas, where goals for both preventing extinction and recovery can be advanced"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How big is the combined footprint, and where is it?", "url": "faq.html#footprint-size", "text": "Across all groups, the recovery footprint covers 492 planning units, about 1,050 km². The priority footprint narrows this to 216 planning units (457 km²), about 43 percent of the full footprint. The footprints span all four islands, centered on upland Haleakalā, West Maui, East Molokaʻi, and Lānaʻihale. About 66 percent lies within biodiversity reserves, and about half already has some ungulate fe"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "What is a planning unit, and do its lines limit where managers can work?", "url": "faq.html#planning-units", "text": "A planning unit (PU) is the basic map unit of the plan. Maui Nui was divided into 1,400 planning units of about 2 km² each, drawn along watersheds, natural barriers, and elevation bands. Each is sized to support one population of an at-risk species. Planning units are units of analysis, not property or management boundaries. The plan maps habitat and actions at the planning-unit level and leaves e"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How were the footprint areas chosen?", "url": "faq.html#how-chosen", "text": "Each species was given a recovery target: how many populations it needs and how they should be spread across islands and mountains. Targets came from existing recovery plans with added expert input. For Plants, Terrestrial arthropods, and Terrestrial snails, a computer program (OptimTFE) generated many possible footprints that each met every species' targets. Experts and managers compared them on "}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Why isn't all suitable habitat included in the footprint?", "url": "faq.html#why-not-all-habitat", "text": "A recovery footprint is built to meet each species' recovery targets, meaning the number and spread of populations it needs. It does not try to include every place a species could live. The goal is a set of planning units that together meet the targets of all species, and smaller footprints let managers concentrate their resources without giving up habitat quality or access. Habitat outside the fo"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How reliable are the species location data behind the footprints?", "url": "faq.html#data-reliability", "text": "Taxon experts checked all occurrence data in collaborative review sessions and flagged errors, outdated records, and naming problems. For Plants, mapped populations were used where available, and checked occurrence records otherwise. Every footprint keeps species-level detail, including whether each choice rests on occurrence records or on model predictions, and the Adaptive Conservation Planner s"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Will the plan move species to places where they never lived?", "url": "faq.html#historic-range", "text": "Recovery targets are placed within each species' historic range on Maui Nui. The rules for choosing footprints required spreading populations across the historic range. Snail exclosure targets sit within each species' historical range, and arthropod targets are spread across each species' historic range. Returning species to places where they once lived is a normal recovery step and usually comes "}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Why does the plan favor lands already managed for conservation?", "url": "faq.html#why-conservation-lands", "text": "Recovery needs long-term investment: fences, field crews, rodent control, and exclosures. So the plan focuses actions, when possible, on lands already managed or set aside for conservation or covered by conservation agreements with private landowners. One of the criteria for choosing footprints was overlap with biodiversity reserves : lands with a lasting conservation commitment, mainly the Forest"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "What conservation actions does the plan recommend?", "url": "faq.html#what-actions", "text": "The action prioritization team compared the expected benefit, feasibility, and cost of 27 strategies (17 single-focus strategies and 10 combinations) for 17 species groups. The key results: No single strategy is enough. Two combinations, S25 (focused on plants and invertebrates) and S27 (focused on birds and the bat), come closest to meeting every group's needs when done together. Between them the"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Why are \"new tools\" such a big part of the plan?", "url": "faq.html#new-tools", "text": "Some of the most serious threats cannot yet be controlled across whole landscapes. Future tools for rare plant and invertebrate conservation (strategy S13) ranked among the top three actions for 13 of the 17 species groups. Forest birds depend on new ways to control mosquito-borne avian malaria, and Terrestrial snails depend on controlling the rosy wolfsnail across whole landscapes. The plan calls"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Does the plan say which actions to take in a particular planning unit?", "url": "faq.html#site-actions", "text": "Not directly. The action prioritization compared strategies for 17 species groups across Maui Nui as a whole, not for individual planning units. The footprints tell managers where to work. The group strategies, the action sequence, and the list of species facing imminent extinction tell them what to do and when. Choosing the right mix for a single site is still a manager's call. For example, a man"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "In what order should actions happen?", "url": "faq.html#sequence", "text": "Emergency actions for the 163 species facing imminent extinction come first. After that, taxon expert teams recommended a sequence for each group. Start with the highest-benefit actions and the groundwork, such as fencing, ungulate removal, and weed control, that sets the stage for later reintroductions. Take up cost-effective, field-ready actions as opportunities arise. Some actions work much bet"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How much will carrying out the plan cost?", "url": "faq.html#cost", "text": "The action prioritization analysis estimates that preventing the extinction of all at-risk species in Maui Nui over the next 20 years would cost, on average, about $55 million a year. A large share would come in the first year, for field vehicles, propagation and rehabilitation facilities, research and development, and fence building. The analysis also estimates an upfront investment of about $67 "}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "What are kīpuka kāhuli?", "url": "faq.html#kipuka-kahuli", "text": "Kīpuka kāhuli are snail exclosures: managed refuges, free of predators, enclosed by a predator-proof fence that keeps out invasive predators such as the rosy wolfsnail, rats, and chameleons. The plan calls for a network of 32 kīpuka kāhuli across Maui Nui's range of climates, protecting at least three populations of each of the 49 at-risk snail species. A priority set of 14 sites gives every speci"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How does the plan help decide where to build fences?", "url": "faq.html#fencing", "text": "Ungulate fencing is groundwork for most groups, and about half of the combined recovery footprint already has some fencing. By laying the footprints over existing fences, managers can see which recovery planning units are unfenced and which species a new or extended fence line would help. The ecosystem analysis also lists unfenced high-quality habitat already in conservation status as a priority f"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "What is the Adaptive Conservation Planner?", "url": "faq.html#what-acp", "text": "The Adaptive Conservation Planner (ACP, also called the Conservation Planner) is the online map tool that holds the plan's final map products: the recovery footprints plus information on species, habitats, and threats. Authorized users can view footprints for any group and click a planning unit to see which species are targeted there and whether their targets are met. They can also propose and rev"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Can anyone use the Adaptive Conservation Planner? How do I get access?", "url": "faq.html#acp-access", "text": "The ACP is not a public web map. It is the internal, editable tool where DOFAW staff, taxon experts, and managers inspect, refine, and maintain the conservation footprints, and it needs a user account. Land managers and agency or partner staff who would use it for planning can ask DOFAW Maui for an account. DOFAW decides which level of access to grant. [TBD: contact address.] Members of the public"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Who can change the footprints, and how are changes approved?", "url": "faq.html#who-edits", "text": "Users can propose edits: move a species target to a different planning unit, add new target locations, or remove targets. Edits can be saved as private drafts, shared for team review, and then submitted for administrator approval. The ACP keeps a record of how and why each change was made. The ACP has been tested through full edit, review, and approval cycles, including expert fine-tuning of the p"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "What if conditions change, for example a new population is found or a site is lost to fire?", "url": "faq.html#field-changes", "text": "That is what the adaptive part of the planner is for. The plan is meant to be updated by managers as circumstances, funding, opportunities, and information change. In the ACP, users can propose moving or adding targets. They can also attach notes to species or planning units that record field observations, access problems, land-tenure information, or changes needed later. The ACP keeps a dated off"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How often will the footprints and the plan be updated?", "url": "faq.html#update-cadence", "text": "The ACP is designed so that future annual reviews can change specific planning units while keeping a traceable record of changes. The plan document itself is subject to periodic review and revision. DOFAW expects to review proposed footprint changes once a year with partners and taxon experts, and to fold accepted changes into the official footprint. The plan's monitoring guidance suggests checkin"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Can I use ACP outputs in my own GIS work, management plans, or grant proposals?", "url": "faq.html#export-use", "text": "Yes. Footprint data can be exported as CSV, GeoPackage, or GeoJSON for use in GIS and other planning systems. The ACP is meant to work alongside DOFAW's Esri-based field systems, not replace them. The plan is designed to help managers build work plans for individual sites, estimate costs, coordinate across agencies and landowners using shared data, and justify funding. For any area, the ACP shows "}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "My team can't work everywhere. How do I narrow the footprint to a starting point?", "url": "faq.html#narrow-footprint", "text": "Use the nested footprints and filters. For Plants, the full recovery footprint has 317 planning units for 195 species. The priority footprint narrows this to 155 units. Limiting it to PEPP species gives 98 units. PEPP species with three or fewer known occupied units need 39, and PEPP species with only one known occupied unit need 12. The plan suggests starting with that last set and moving to the "}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Does the ACP reveal where rare species occur?", "url": "faq.html#sensitive-data", "text": "Exact locations of rare species are sensitive. The plan maps habitat and actions at the level of planning units of about 2 km², not exact sites. Detailed occurrence records in the ACP are limited to a small number of administrator-level users and are never shown by default. Data shared by partners stay under the terms of their data-sharing agreements."}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Can the tools help pick exact planting or exclosure sites? What else is planned?", "url": "faq.html#other-tools", "text": "The plan includes Find My Microhabitat , a companion tool that zooms in from a planning unit to the specific spots best suited for outplanting, siting exclosures, surveys, or restoration. It uses fine-scale data such as vegetation structure, canopy cover, terrain, and moisture. PEPP partners already use it to choose outplanting sites, and it is being documented for wider use. Three more tools are "}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Is the MNLCP a regulation?", "url": "faq.html#regulatory", "text": "No. The MNLCP is a decision-support plan that gives flexible guidance to managers. It is built on partnership, not on prescriptive mandates. It does not create new rules, permits, or land-use restrictions. Existing protections for listed species under state and federal law are unchanged, and the plan works within them."}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Part of my land is inside a footprint. What does that mean for me?", "url": "faq.html#my-land", "text": "A footprint shows where habitat that could help recover at-risk species exists or could be restored. It is a planning map, not a designation, and it places no requirement on a landowner. Most of the footprint, about 66 percent, is on biodiversity reserves. Where footprint areas fall on other lands, the plan's approach is to build partnerships with private landowners, communities, and agencies to s"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How can private landowners take part?", "url": "faq.html#private-landowners", "text": "The plan calls for partnerships with private landowners, communities, and agencies to promote recovery and habitat management. Its ecosystem priorities include working with landowners and partners where high-quality native habitat lies outside conservation status. Examples include eastern Molokaʻi, the area above Makawao, and Kahikinui. Recommended actions include working with private landowners o"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How does the plan relate to federal critical habitat or permitting?", "url": "faq.html#critical-habitat", "text": "The plan does not designate critical habitat. That is a separate federal process under the Endangered Species Act, and reintroductions still go through their usual reviews. USFWS was a partner in developing the plan. The recovery footprints and the data behind them can inform federal and state recovery work. They were built for management, though, and weigh practical factors such as accessibility,"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Does the plan help guide land protection or acquisition?", "url": "faq.html#acquisition", "text": "Yes. One stated use of the plan is to help identify lands that should be managed under long-term protection. The ecosystem analysis maps where high-quality native habitat lies outside conservation status and is unfenced. It labels such areas \"Engage: management possible if land is secured.\" For any parcel of interest, the ACP can list the at-risk species targeted in its planning units. That helps "}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How does the plan account for climate change?", "url": "faq.html#climate", "text": "In several ways. Climate resilience was one of the criteria for choosing footprints. It was estimated from how fast temperature and rainfall have actually changed in the past, rather than from uncertain future projections. Plant recovery areas were built with climate-suitability models, and snail habitat with climate-similarity models. The resulting footprints generally have better-than-average cl"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Does the plan consider wildfire?", "url": "faq.html#fire", "text": "Yes. Fire risk was one of the criteria for choosing footprints, because lower fire risk reduces the chance of losing conservation investments. Fire management is among the highest-priority actions for Plants. Building firebreaks is one of the recommended habitat management actions, and firebreaks can be one of the first steps before reintroducing a species."}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Why does the plan include ecosystems as well as species?", "url": "faq.html#why-ecosystems", "text": "Healthy native ecosystems keep more species from becoming at risk and give recovering species room to expand. They provide connections between populations, buffers against threats, backup populations, and space to grow. The plan maps ten ecosystems, plus special features such as anchialine pools, lava tubes, bogs, and cliffs. Some ecosystems are nearly gone: lowland dry forest, once the most exten"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "What are the plan's ecosystem priorities?", "url": "faq.html#ecosystem-priorities", "text": "The plan sets four ecosystem priorities, in order: Maintain high-quality habitat that is already fenced and managed. Start ungulate management on unfenced high-quality land already in conservation status, such as windward Haleakalā, two areas of Molokaʻi, and Lānaʻihale. Work with landowners and partners where high-quality habitat lies outside conservation status, including eastern Molokaʻi, the a"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How does the plan consider cultural values?", "url": "faq.html#cultural-values", "text": "The plan recognizes that Hawaiʻi's native species and habitats are closely tied to the state's biocultural heritage. These ecosystems are the foundation of cultural practices, natural resource management, and local economies. The plan's third goal is to protect and restore native ecosystems so they keep sustaining clean water, cultural values, and local economies. Its planning approach is built on"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How will progress be measured?", "url": "faq.html#measure-progress", "text": "Each species has population targets assigned to specific planning units. So monitoring can ask the same question everywhere: is the population in each targeted unit moving toward the numbers and spread the plan calls for? The answers feed adaptive management, a learning cycle of collecting monitoring data, evaluating it, and using the results to decide whether the location, type, or intensity of m"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Is this the final plan? Will it change?", "url": "faq.html#is-final", "text": "The plan document is a dated version, September 29, 2026, and is subject to periodic review and revision. The conservation footprints in the Adaptive Conservation Planner are the living version that managers use day to day. A stable, dated official footprint is kept alongside them for reporting. The plan is meant to be updated as conditions, funding, opportunities, and knowledge change."}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Is the plan funded? What will it take to succeed?", "url": "faq.html#what-success-requires", "text": "The plan sets out what is needed. Carrying it out depends on sustained funding, staff, and partnerships. It calls on leaders and partners to: fund emergency actions now for the 163 species that could be lost within five years; invest in new tools for the hardest threats and for captive rearing and propagation; sustain and expand groundwork such as fencing, ungulate removal, weed and predator contr"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Will this approach be used on other islands?", "url": "faq.html#other-islands", "text": "The plan is meant to serve as a model for other islands and programs. The action prioritization process was designed so it can be applied to other islands in the state. Planned improvements to the ACP include documentation for use beyond Maui Nui."}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "I have new information about an at-risk species. How can I share it or get involved?", "url": "faq.html#share-info", "text": "New observations matter. The footprints are designed to take in new population discoveries, field knowledge, and changed conditions. Records of at-risk species, especially populations not yet known, can help fill data gaps for the 143 species that cannot yet be mapped. Please send observations to DOFAW Maui Branch. [TBD: contact address and preferred format.] Land managers and partner organization"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "How were communities and Native Hawaiian organizations involved?", "url": "faq.html#community", "text": "DOFAW Maui developed the plan with a broad coalition of public and private agencies, organizations, landowners, and technical experts. The approach is built on partnership and on respect for local communities and cultures, and the plan calls for building partnerships with communities as it is carried out. [TBD: describe community and Native Hawaiian engagement to date and how it will continue duri"}, {"page": "Q&A", "title": "Where can I get the full plan and the maps?", "url": "faq.html#get-plan", "text": "The full plan is available from the Resources page. The combined footprints can be explored on the Conservation Footprints map , and the species on the At-Risk Species page. [TBD: confirm the plan PDF is cleared for public download, and which map files (such as the KMZ) can be shared.]"}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Action Prioritization Team", "url": "glossary.html#action-prioritization-team", "text": "The UH Mānoa–led team that addressed the “what to do” portion of the MNLCP. Using a modified Priority Threat Management process, the team worked with roughly 100 land managers and scientists to identify key threats, evaluate conservation actions, and estimate the costs and benefits of management strategies for Maui Nui’s at-risk species."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Adaptive Conservation Planner (ACP)", "url": "glossary.html#adaptive-conservation-planner-acp", "text": "Called the “Conservation Planner” for short, this is the spatially oriented, adaptive analytic tool that houses the MNLCP’s final spatial products—including recovery footprints and species, habitat, and threat metadata—and allows managers to revise strategies, develop annual work plans, and explore management scenarios."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Algorithmic Pathway", "url": "glossary.html#algorithmic-pathway", "text": "One of the two process pathways used in spatial conservation planning, alongside the Expert-Led Pathway. Applied to taxonomic groups with numerous species and complex habitat or management considerations (Plants, Terrestrial arthropods, Terrestrial snails), it used the OptimTFE algorithm to generate candidate footprints across Maui Nui’s 1,400 planning units, which experts and managers then compared against criteria such as habitat quality, accessibility, and climate resilience."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "At-Risk Species", "url": "glossary.html#at-risk-species", "text": "A species identified by the Taxonomic Teams and other experts—using existing endangered species lists, Hawaiʻi’s State Wildlife Action Plan, and other sources—as likely to become extinct within the next 20 years without significant management intervention. The MNLCP identifies 442 at-risk species of birds, plants, invertebrates, and a bat, driven by factors such as low remaining population numbers, climate change, invasive species, and habitat loss."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Chapter 195D, Hawaiʻi Revised Statutes", "url": "glossary.html#chapter-195d-hawaiʻi-revised-statutes", "text": "Titled “Conservation of Aquatic Life, Wildlife, and Land Plants,” Chapter 195D is the State’s primary legal framework for protecting endangered and threatened species. It is administered by the Hawaiʻi Department of Land and Natural Resources (DLNR)."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Communications Team", "url": "glossary.html#communications-team", "text": "The team that develops and implements communication strategies to inform the public and others about the MNLCP and its accomplishments."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Core Team", "url": "glossary.html#core-team", "text": "The DOFAW-led team that oversees MNLCP development, scheduling, and coordination of the other MNLCP teams."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Endangered Species", "url": "glossary.html#endangered-species", "text": "A species formally listed under federal or State law as being in danger of extinction (shown as “E” in the plan’s ESA status tables), as distinct from a threatened species."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Endangered Species Act (ESA)", "url": "glossary.html#endangered-species-act-esa", "text": "The federal law under which species are formally listed as endangered or threatened. The plan tracks each at-risk species’ ESA status (E = endangered, T = threatened) throughout its taxonomic group tables."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Expert-Led Pathway", "url": "glossary.html#expert-led-pathway", "text": "One of the two process pathways used in spatial conservation planning, alongside the Algorithmic Pathway. Used for taxonomic groups with fewer focal species or where expert knowledge was more informative than algorithmic methods (Forest birds, Seabirds, Waterbirds, Aquatic invertebrates), it had specialists identify priority areas directly through iterative web-map review."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Ex situ", "url": "glossary.html#ex-situ", "text": "Conservation actions carried out outside a species’ natural habitat, such as captive rearing, captive breeding, or seed storage and banking."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Hawaiʻi State Wildlife Action Plan (SWAP)", "url": "glossary.html#hawaiʻi-state-wildlife-action-plan-swap", "text": "One of the existing State planning documents the MNLCP draws on as a data source—alongside endangered species lists, conservation databases, and the literature—to help identify Maui Nui’s at-risk species."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Implementation Team", "url": "glossary.html#implementation-team", "text": "The DOFAW-led team of land managers, planners, and decision-makers responsible for the “when to do it” guidance—translating the results of the spatial prioritization, action prioritization, and ecosystem prioritization efforts into a final, cohesive implementation plan, and overseeing development of the Managers’ Handbook."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "In situ", "url": "glossary.html#in-situ", "text": "Conservation actions carried out within a species’ natural habitat or wild population, such as predator-proof exclosures or direct protection and management of existing wild populations, as distinguished from ex situ actions performed away from the wild."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Integrated Multi-Scale Conservation Planning (IMCP)", "url": "glossary.html#integrated-multi-scale-conservation-planning-imcp", "text": "The MNLCP’s overarching planning approach, combining spatial prioritization, action prioritization, and ecosystem planning into a single, adaptive decision-support system. IMCP is state-led and expert-engaged, integrates conservation across biological, spatial, and temporal scales, and is oriented toward decision support and partnership rather than prescriptive mandates."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Maui Nui", "url": "glossary.html#maui-nui", "text": "The landscape covered by the plan, encompassing the islands of Maui, Molokaʻi, Lānaʻi, and Kahoʻolawe."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "OptimTFE", "url": "glossary.html#optimtfe", "text": "The site-selection spatial prioritization algorithm used within the TFE-SCP process. It generated hundreds of thousands of candidate recovery footprints, each meeting species-specific recovery or extinction-prevention targets, for comparison by species experts and land managers."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Persistence", "url": "glossary.html#persistence", "text": "The probability that a species group will have viable, self-sustaining populations over a 20-year period. It is the central metric used to evaluate the expected benefit of different conservation action strategies in the Priority Threat Management analysis."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Planning Unit (PU)", "url": "glossary.html#planning-unit-pu", "text": "The basic spatial unit used in the MNLCP’s spatial prioritization. The Maui Nui landscape was divided into a network of 1,400 medium-sized planning units, each roughly 2 km², delineated using watersheds, natural barriers, and elevational bands. Recovery and priority footprints are built up as collections of planning units, and each unit is sized to support a single population of an at-risk species. Finer-scale siting of specific actions within a planning unit (such as exactly where to outplant or fence) is then worked out by field teams using additional tools."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Priority Footprint", "url": "glossary.html#priority-footprint", "text": "The subset of a recovery footprint identifying the highest-priority planning units for near-term conservation action, which can be further narrowed into more focused annual work objectives. For example, the Plants recovery footprint’s 317 planning units include a priority footprint of 155 planning units."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Priority Threat Management (PTM)", "url": "glossary.html#priority-threat-management-ptm", "text": "A modified structured decision-making process used by the Action Prioritization Team to evaluate the expected costs, feasibility, and conservation benefits of management strategies. It assessed 27 conservation strategies (17 single-focus, 10 combinations) to identify the most cost-effective ways to raise species groups’ likelihood of persistence."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Recovery", "url": "glossary.html#recovery", "text": "Restoring an at-risk species to a healthy, self-sustaining population level. The MNLCP considers a species no longer at risk of extinction once it reaches its established recovery targets, even if it remains reliant on some ongoing conservation actions (“conservation reliant”)."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Recovery Footprint", "url": "glossary.html#recovery-footprint", "text": "The spatially explicit planning units identified by the Spatial Prioritization Team and Taxonomic Teams as containing existing or restorable habitat needed for an at-risk species, or taxonomic group, to reach its recovery goals."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Spatial Prioritization", "url": "glossary.html#spatial-prioritization", "text": "The planning effort, implemented through the TFE-SCP process, that identifies where on the landscape to focus conservation efforts to meet explicit species recovery targets. It addresses the plan’s “where to manage” question."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Spatial Prioritization Team", "url": "glossary.html#spatial-prioritization-team", "text": "The USGS-led team responsible for the spatial prioritization effort, addressing “where to manage” for the recovery of Maui Nui’s at-risk species using the TFE-SCP process."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Taxonomic Team", "url": "glossary.html#taxonomic-team", "text": "DOFAW-led teams of species specialists that formed the backbone of the planning effort, providing qualitative input on species distributions, habitat quality, conservation needs, and management feasibility. Seven Taxonomic Teams were established (forest bird, seabird, waterbird, plant, snail, aquatic invertebrate, and terrestrial arthropod teams); the Hawaiian hoary bat is an eighth taxonomic group without its own dedicated team."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Threatened Species", "url": "glossary.html#threatened-species", "text": "A species formally listed under federal or State law as likely to become endangered in the foreseeable future (shown as “T” in the plan’s ESA status tables), as distinct from a species already listed as endangered. For example, ʻiʻiwi is federally listed as threatened, and nēnē is federally threatened while being state-listed as endangered."}, {"page": "Glossary", "title": "Transparent, Flexible, Expert-engaged Systematic Conservation Planning (TFE-SCP)", "url": "glossary.html#transparent-flexible-expert-engaged-systematic-conservation-planning-tfe-scp", "text": "The USGS-developed spatial planning process used to implement spatial prioritization in the MNLCP. It is a comprehensive planning process with distinct stages, each supported by custom-built tools and involving input from biological experts and land managers, emphasizing transparency in decision-making, flexibility to adapt to different data and management contexts, and deep expert engagement throughout."}, {"page": "Home", "title": "Home", "url": "index.html", "text": "The challenge One of the most severe extinction crises in the nation With less than 0.2 percent of the nation's land area, Hawaiʻi accounts for 29 percent of its imperiled species. More than 60 percent of Hawaiʻi's native ecosystems have been destroyed or highly degraded. Many native species now survive only in small, scattered populations in high-elevation refuges or fenced areas. Invasive predators, ungulates (hoofed animals such as goats, deer, and cattle), weeds, disease, fire, and climate change continue to drive declines. Many good plans already exist, but they are not integrated at the "}];
